QUESTIONS.push(

  // ================= E-Governance Models & Digital India (50) =================
  { id: "gov-01", topic: "eg-models", q: "E-Governance is best defined as:", options: ["Only the sale of goods online by government agencies", "The use of information and communication technology (ICT) by government to deliver services, share information, and engage citizens more efficiently and transparently", "A synonym for e-commerce with no distinction", "Only the digitization of government office furniture and buildings"], answer: 1, exp: "E-Governance is broader than a single service — it covers service delivery, information sharing, and governance processes conducted through ICT." },
  { id: "gov-02", topic: "eg-models", q: "E-Government, as distinct from the broader term e-Governance, typically refers more narrowly to:", options: ["Only private-sector online business", "The use of ICT specifically by government agencies to deliver public services and information, a subset of the broader e-Governance concept (which also includes governance processes, participation, and policy)", "A synonym for e-Governance with no distinction whatsoever", "Only physical government buildings"], answer: 1, exp: "e-Government often emphasizes service delivery; e-Governance is broader, also covering governance, participation, and policy dimensions." },
  { id: "gov-03", topic: "eg-models", q: "In the 'roles of Commerce, Business, and Governance' framework relevant to Digital India, 'Commerce (C)' primarily refers to:", options: ["Government policy-making processes", "Buying and selling of goods/services, especially conducted electronically (e-commerce)", "Only non-profit charitable activity", "Only manufacturing processes"], answer: 1, exp: "Commerce centers on transactions — the exchange of goods/services for value, increasingly conducted digitally." },
  { id: "gov-04", topic: "eg-models", q: "In this framework, 'Business (B)' refers more broadly to:", options: ["Only the buying/selling transaction itself", "The full range of an organization's processes and operations (which may include but go beyond commerce), such as production, HR, and internal management, increasingly digitized", "Only activities performed by the government", "Only individual citizen activities"], answer: 1, exp: "Business is broader than commerce — it includes internal organizational processes beyond just transactions." },
  { id: "gov-05", topic: "eg-models", q: "In this framework, 'Governance (G)' refers to:", options: ["Only private company management", "How government exercises authority and delivers public services, increasingly through digital means (e-Governance) to improve efficiency, transparency and citizen engagement", "A synonym for commerce with no distinction", "Only judicial court proceedings"], answer: 1, exp: "Governance encompasses the exercise of public authority and administration, a role distinct from private commerce/business." },
  { id: "gov-06", topic: "eg-models", q: "The Digital India initiative is best described as:", options: ["A private company's internal IT project", "A Government of India program aimed at transforming India into a digitally empowered society and knowledge economy through improved digital infrastructure, governance, and services", "A single mobile application with no broader program behind it", "An initiative limited only to urban areas"], answer: 1, exp: "Digital India is a national flagship program spanning infrastructure, digital service delivery, and digital literacy/empowerment." },
  { id: "gov-07", topic: "eg-models", q: "Among the stated goals of Digital India is:", options: ["Reducing internet connectivity nationwide", "Providing universal digital infrastructure as a core utility, delivering governance and services on demand, and achieving digital empowerment of citizens", "Only benefiting large corporations, with no citizen focus", "Eliminating all government-citizen digital interaction"], answer: 1, exp: "These three broad pillars (infrastructure, governance/services on demand, digital empowerment) frame the Digital India vision." },
  { id: "gov-08", topic: "eg-models", q: "G2G (Government-to-Government) e-governance interactions refer to:", options: ["Interactions between a government and its private-sector vendors only", "Electronic interactions/data sharing between different government departments, agencies, or levels of government (e.g., state to central)", "Interactions solely between citizens and government", "A type of business-to-business e-commerce"], answer: 1, exp: "G2G improves coordination and information sharing across the machinery of government itself." },
  { id: "gov-09", topic: "eg-models", q: "C2C (Citizen-to-Citizen) interactions in an e-governance/digital context refer to:", options: ["Interactions exclusively between government departments", "Electronic interactions directly between citizens/individuals, such as peer-to-peer online marketplaces or community platforms, sometimes facilitated or regulated within a broader digital ecosystem", "Only interactions between a business and its own employees", "A term with no practical meaning in e-governance"], answer: 1, exp: "C2C highlights that digital ecosystems also enable citizen-to-citizen exchanges, which governance frameworks may need to account for (e.g., online marketplaces, dispute resolution)." },
  { id: "gov-10", topic: "eg-models", q: "C2B (Citizen-to-Business) interactions refer to:", options: ["A business selling directly to government only", "Individuals/citizens providing value, information, or services to businesses electronically (e.g., a citizen submitting a product review, or offering freelance services via a platform)", "Only government regulating businesses", "A synonym for B2C with no distinction"], answer: 1, exp: "C2B reverses the traditional direction — here the individual is the initiator providing something of value to the business." },
  { id: "gov-11", topic: "eg-models", q: "C2G (Citizen-to-Government) interactions refer to:", options: ["A business paying taxes to the government", "Citizens electronically interacting with government, such as filing complaints, paying taxes, submitting applications, or participating in e-consultations", "Only government issuing instructions to citizens with no citizen-initiated interaction", "A type of citizen-to-citizen transaction"], answer: 1, exp: "C2G captures citizen-initiated digital engagement with government services and processes." },
  { id: "gov-12", topic: "eg-models", q: "B2C (Business-to-Consumer/Citizen) interactions refer to:", options: ["Businesses interacting only with other businesses", "A business selling goods/services directly to individual citizens/consumers, typically the most familiar e-commerce model", "Government departments interacting with each other", "Only citizens interacting with other citizens"], answer: 1, exp: "B2C is the most common e-commerce model, e.g., an online retailer selling directly to individual customers." },
  { id: "gov-13", topic: "eg-models", q: "Beyond G2G, C2C, C2B, C2G and B2C, another commonly recognized e-governance interaction model is:", options: ["G2C (Government-to-Citizen), where government delivers services/information directly to citizens electronically", "There are no other recognized interaction models", "Only physical, in-person interactions count as valid models", "A model exclusively for military use"], answer: 0, exp: "G2C (and similarly G2B, Government-to-Business) are additional standard interaction models alongside the ones explicitly highlighted." },
  { id: "gov-14", topic: "eg-models", q: "A 'collaborative API' in the context of Digital India/e-governance refers to:", options: ["An API that only one single organization is ever allowed to use", "A programmatic interface that lets multiple stakeholders (government, businesses, developers) securely access and build upon a shared digital service or dataset", "A physical hardware connector", "A type of encryption algorithm"], answer: 1, exp: "APIs like those in India Stack (Aadhaar, UPI, DigiLocker) enable an ecosystem of third-party applications to be built on shared government digital infrastructure." },
  { id: "gov-15", topic: "eg-models", q: "'Collaborative application development' in e-governance typically involves:", options: ["A single government department building every application entirely alone with no outside involvement", "Government, private sector, developers, and sometimes citizens jointly contributing to building digital public applications/solutions, often via open APIs, hackathons, or open-source projects", "Only foreign companies developing applications for India with no domestic involvement", "Avoiding any use of modern software development practices"], answer: 1, exp: "This collaborative model accelerates innovation and broadens the ecosystem of applications built on public digital infrastructure." },
  { id: "gov-16", topic: "eg-models", q: "A 'collaborative digital platform', as seen in initiatives like UMANG or DigiLocker, is characterized by:", options: ["Serving only a single, narrow government function with no integration", "Bringing together multiple government services, departments, or stakeholders onto one shared, interoperable platform accessible to citizens/businesses", "Being accessible only to government employees, never citizens", "Requiring users to visit a physical office to access any service"], answer: 1, exp: "Such platforms consolidate diverse services into a unified citizen-facing (or business-facing) digital experience." },
  { id: "gov-17", topic: "eg-models", q: "UMANG (Unified Mobile Application for New-age Governance) is an example of:", options: ["A single-purpose private banking app", "A collaborative digital platform that integrates multiple central and state government services into a single mobile application for citizens", "A hardware device for government offices", "A programming language used to build government websites"], answer: 1, exp: "UMANG exemplifies the collaborative digital platform model, consolidating many government services into one citizen-facing app." },
  { id: "gov-18", topic: "eg-models", q: "DigiLocker, another Digital India platform, primarily provides citizens with:", options: ["A physical safe deposit box service", "A secure digital space to store, access, and share verified official documents (like certificates and licenses) electronically", "A social media networking service", "An online shopping marketplace"], answer: 1, exp: "DigiLocker reduces reliance on physical document copies by providing authenticated digital access to official records." },
  { id: "gov-19", topic: "eg-models", q: "India Stack, a set of open APIs underpinning much of India's digital public infrastructure, notably includes:", options: ["Only a single proprietary payment app with no broader API ecosystem", "Components such as Aadhaar-based identity verification and the Unified Payments Interface (UPI), enabling a broad ecosystem of applications to be built on shared digital infrastructure", "Only offline, paper-based government forms", "A hardware manufacturing standard"], answer: 1, exp: "India Stack's layered, open API approach (identity, payments, data) is a widely cited model for digital public infrastructure." },
  { id: "gov-20", topic: "eg-models", q: "A key advantage of a collaborative, API-driven approach to digital governance (as opposed to each department building isolated, closed systems) is:", options: ["It guarantees zero security risk with no additional considerations needed", "It enables faster innovation, interoperability, and reuse of shared digital infrastructure across many applications and services", "It eliminates the need for any citizen data protection", "It requires every citizen to personally write their own software"], answer: 1, exp: "Shared, well-designed APIs let many different applications and services build on common, reusable digital infrastructure rather than duplicating effort." },
  { id: "gov-21", topic: "eg-models", q: "G2B (Government-to-Business) interactions refer to:", options: ["Government interacting only with individual citizens, never businesses", "Government providing services, information, and regulatory interactions (like licensing, tax filing, or procurement) to businesses electronically", "A synonym for C2B with no distinction", "Only businesses selling products to government employees personally"], answer: 1, exp: "G2B streamlines processes like business registration, compliance filing, and government procurement through digital channels." },
  { id: "gov-22", topic: "eg-models", q: "A key motivation for the 'Digital India' program's emphasis on digital infrastructure as a 'core utility' is:", options: ["Digital connectivity is considered a luxury with no essential public value", "Reliable digital access (like electricity or water) is increasingly seen as foundational to enabling citizens to access essential services, information, and opportunities", "Only wealthy citizens should have access to digital infrastructure", "Physical infrastructure should be entirely replaced with no digital component"], answer: 1, exp: "Framing digital access as a core utility reflects its foundational role in modern participation in government services, education, and the economy." },
  { id: "gov-23", topic: "eg-models", q: "'Services on demand', a Digital India objective, envisions:", options: ["Citizens being required to visit a government office in person for every single service", "Government services being available electronically, seamlessly, and in real time across departments/jurisdictions, integrated so citizens don't need to navigate fragmented bureaucratic silos", "Services only being available once per year on a fixed date", "Eliminating all government services entirely"], answer: 1, exp: "The 'services on demand' pillar aims for citizen-centric, integrated, anytime-anywhere access to government services." },
  { id: "gov-24", topic: "eg-models", q: "Digital empowerment of citizens, as a Digital India goal, includes objectives such as:", options: ["Restricting citizens' access to digital tools and information", "Universal digital literacy, availability of digital resources/services in local languages, and ensuring citizens can meaningfully participate in and benefit from the digital ecosystem", "Only benefiting citizens who already have advanced technical skills", "Eliminating the need for any citizen participation in governance"], answer: 1, exp: "Digital empowerment aims to make digital participation genuinely accessible and beneficial to the broadest possible population, not just the already tech-savvy." },
  { id: "gov-25", topic: "eg-models", q: "Which best distinguishes e-commerce from e-governance?", options: ["They are identical concepts describing the same activities", "E-commerce centers on commercial transactions (buying/selling); e-governance centers on the use of ICT for government administration, service delivery, and citizen engagement — though both can overlap, e.g., online tax payment", "E-governance only applies to private companies, never government", "E-commerce only applies to government agencies, never private companies"], answer: 1, exp: "While related in their use of digital technology, these terms describe distinct domains — commercial transactions versus government administration/service delivery." },
  { id: "gov-26", topic: "eg-models", q: "Interoperability between different e-governance interaction models (G2G, G2C, G2B, etc.) is important because:", options: ["Each model should operate in complete isolation with no data sharing ever", "Citizens and businesses often need seamless, integrated experiences that may span multiple government departments/agencies, requiring underlying systems to share data and work together effectively", "Interoperability has no practical relevance to e-governance", "Only G2G interactions ever require any interoperability"], answer: 1, exp: "A citizen applying for a service, for example, may require data/verification flowing seamlessly across several government departments behind the scenes." },
  { id: "gov-27", topic: "eg-models", q: "An 'open API' approach to government digital infrastructure, as used in India Stack, primarily benefits the broader ecosystem by:", options: ["Restricting all access to a single private company", "Allowing a wide range of third-party developers, startups, and organizations to build innovative applications and services on top of shared government digital infrastructure", "Preventing any innovation outside of government itself", "Requiring every application to be built exclusively by the government"], answer: 1, exp: "Open APIs turn foundational digital infrastructure into a platform that a much broader ecosystem of innovators can build upon." },
  { id: "gov-28", topic: "eg-models", q: "A well-designed e-governance interaction model (e.g., G2C service delivery) should generally prioritize:", options: ["Maximizing bureaucratic complexity for citizens", "Simplicity, accessibility, transparency, and efficiency from the citizen's perspective", "Making services accessible only through paper forms", "Requiring citizens to understand internal government department structures before accessing any service"], answer: 1, exp: "Citizen-centric design — making services simple and accessible regardless of internal government complexity — is a core e-governance design principle." },
  { id: "gov-29", topic: "eg-models", q: "The transition from purely paper-based government processes to digital G2C/G2B service delivery is primarily motivated by the goal of:", options: ["Making government services slower and less accessible", "Improving efficiency, reducing corruption/discretion, increasing transparency, and improving citizen convenience and access", "Eliminating all government accountability", "Increasing the cost of accessing government services for citizens"], answer: 1, exp: "These efficiency, transparency, and accessibility benefits are core justifications for digitizing government service delivery." },
  { id: "gov-30", topic: "eg-models", q: "A key challenge in implementing effective C2G digital interactions (like online grievance filing) is:", options: ["There is no challenge whatsoever in implementing such systems", "Ensuring the system is genuinely accessible (considering digital literacy, language, connectivity) and that citizen input is actually acted upon, not just collected", "Only allowing government employees to use the system, never citizens", "Making the process as bureaucratically complex as possible"], answer: 1, exp: "Genuine citizen empowerment requires both accessible design and a responsive government process behind the digital interface." },
  { id: "gov-31", topic: "eg-models", q: "GSTN (Goods and Services Tax Network), which supports India's GST system, exemplifies which type of e-governance interaction primarily?", options: ["Purely C2C (citizen-to-citizen) with no business or government involvement", "Primarily G2B (Government-to-Business), facilitating tax filing/compliance interactions between businesses and government, alongside supporting G2G data sharing", "Only B2C e-commerce with no government role", "A purely social media platform"], answer: 1, exp: "GSTN's core function is enabling businesses to interact with tax authorities digitally, a classic G2B use case, with underlying G2G data sharing across tax jurisdictions." },
  { id: "gov-32", topic: "eg-models", q: "A key design principle behind collaborative digital platforms consolidating many services (like UMANG) is:", options: ["Forcing citizens to remember dozens of separate apps and logins for each individual government service", "Reducing citizen friction by providing a single, unified access point to many otherwise fragmented government services", "Making each government service deliberately harder to find", "Restricting access to only a single specific government department's services"], answer: 1, exp: "Consolidation directly addresses the fragmentation problem that arises when each department builds its own siloed digital service." },
  { id: "gov-33", topic: "eg-models", q: "'Digital Public Infrastructure' (DPI), a concept closely associated with India Stack, refers to:", options: ["Only privately owned proprietary software with no public access", "Foundational, often open, digital systems (identity, payments, data exchange) built as shared public infrastructure that many applications and services can be built upon, similar in spirit to physical infrastructure like roads", "Only physical server hardware owned by a single company", "A term with no real-world application"], answer: 1, exp: "DPI extends the infrastructure concept into the digital realm — foundational, broadly accessible digital 'rails' that support a wide range of applications." },
  { id: "gov-34", topic: "eg-models", q: "In the C/B/G framework, which of these best illustrates the 'Governance (G)' role specifically (as distinct from Commerce or Business)?", options: ["A private company selling shoes online to consumers", "A state government launching an online portal for citizens to apply for and track welfare scheme benefits", "A company managing its internal supply chain via ERP software", "Two individuals privately buying and selling used goods on a marketplace app"], answer: 1, exp: "The welfare-scheme portal exemplifies government's Governance role: exercising public authority and delivering public services electronically." },
  { id: "gov-35", topic: "eg-models", q: "In the C/B/G framework, which of these best illustrates the 'Commerce (C)' role?", options: ["A government agency publishing a new regulatory policy", "A consumer purchasing a product from an online retailer", "A government department sharing data with another department", "A city government installing smart streetlights"], answer: 1, exp: "This is a straightforward transactional (buying/selling) example — the defining characteristic of Commerce." },
  { id: "gov-36", topic: "eg-models", q: "In the C/B/G framework, which of these best illustrates the 'Business (B)' role, distinct from a narrow commerce transaction?", options: ["A citizen paying a utility bill online", "A manufacturing company digitizing its internal production planning and inventory management processes", "Two government ministries coordinating on a joint policy", "A citizen filing a police complaint online"], answer: 1, exp: "This example goes beyond a simple buy/sell transaction, covering broader internal organizational/operational processes — the hallmark of the 'Business' role." },
  { id: "gov-37", topic: "eg-models", q: "A country pursuing a 'Digital India'-style strategy generally recognizes that Commerce, Business, and Governance are:", options: ["Entirely unrelated domains with no interaction or overlap", "Interconnected domains that all benefit from, and increasingly depend on, shared digital infrastructure, platforms, and interoperable systems", "Domains that should each build completely separate, non-interoperable digital systems", "Concepts relevant only in developed countries, not in a country like India"], answer: 1, exp: "Digital India's strategy recognizes that robust, shared digital infrastructure benefits commerce, business, and governance together, rather than treating them as isolated silos." },
  { id: "gov-38", topic: "eg-models", q: "A key risk of NOT adopting interoperable, collaborative digital platforms across government departments is:", options: ["Improved citizen experience with no downside", "Fragmented, duplicated systems that create friction, inconsistent data, and a poor, confusing experience for citizens who must navigate many disconnected portals", "Automatically improved efficiency with no coordination needed", "No risk at all — isolated systems always work better"], answer: 1, exp: "Without deliberate interoperability efforts, government digitization can simply recreate bureaucratic fragmentation in digital form." },
  { id: "gov-39", topic: "eg-models", q: "Open, collaborative APIs for government digital infrastructure must be balanced against which important consideration?", options: ["No consideration is needed; openness has no trade-offs whatsoever", "Appropriate security, privacy, and access-control safeguards, since broad accessibility must not come at the cost of citizen data protection", "APIs should never be secured in any way", "Only government employees should ever be allowed API access, defeating the purpose of an open ecosystem"], answer: 1, exp: "Openness and accessibility must be carefully balanced with robust security and privacy protections, especially given the sensitivity of citizen data." },
  { id: "gov-40", topic: "eg-models", q: "A 'digital divide' concern relevant to Digital India's citizen-facing platforms refers to:", options: ["A technical term for network latency with no social dimension", "The gap between those who have reliable access to digital technology/connectivity/literacy and those who do not, risking unequal benefit from digital government services", "A type of encryption vulnerability", "A term describing only rural-to-urban migration, unrelated to technology access"], answer: 1, exp: "Addressing the digital divide is essential to ensuring digital governance benefits are broadly and equitably shared, not concentrated among already-advantaged groups." },
  { id: "gov-41", topic: "eg-models", q: "Which best describes why 'collaborative application development' (e.g., via hackathons or open-source government projects) can accelerate digital governance progress?", options: ["It guarantees every resulting application will be flawless with no need for review", "It taps into a wider pool of talent, ideas, and innovation beyond what a single government department could produce alone, often at lower cost and higher speed", "It removes all need for any government oversight or quality control", "It only works for very small, unimportant projects"], answer: 1, exp: "Broader participation can surface more innovative solutions and accelerate development, though appropriate oversight/quality standards still remain important." },
  { id: "gov-42", topic: "eg-models", q: "A citizen using a mobile banking-linked UPI app to pay a friend back for lunch is an example of which interaction model?", options: ["G2G", "C2C (facilitated by a business/technology platform), since it's a peer-to-peer citizen transaction", "G2C", "B2G"], answer: 1, exp: "Even though a private company's app/platform facilitates it, the actual value exchange here is directly between two citizens." },
  { id: "gov-43", topic: "eg-models", q: "A startup using the Aadhaar API (part of India Stack) to verify a new customer's identity during onboarding illustrates:", options: ["A purely internal government process with no business involvement", "A collaborative API enabling private businesses to build services on top of shared government digital identity infrastructure", "An entirely offline, paper-based verification process", "A type of C2C interaction with no business involvement"], answer: 1, exp: "This is a textbook example of a collaborative, open API turning government digital infrastructure into a platform businesses can build upon." },
  { id: "gov-44", topic: "eg-models", q: "Compared to a traditional model where every government department builds its own isolated citizen portal, a unified collaborative platform approach generally offers:", options: ["Worse consistency and a more fragmented citizen experience", "Better consistency, reduced duplication of effort, and a more coherent overall citizen experience across services", "No meaningful difference in outcome", "Only benefits for government employees, none for citizens"], answer: 1, exp: "Unified platforms reduce the redundant effort and inconsistent experience that fragmented, siloed development tends to produce." },
  { id: "gov-45", topic: "eg-models", q: "Which of the following would be classified as a G2G interaction?", options: ["A citizen renewing their driving license online", "A state transport department electronically sharing vehicle registration data with the central road transport database", "A private logistics company delivering packages to homes", "A citizen leaving a product review on an e-commerce site"], answer: 1, exp: "This is an exchange of data/coordination directly between two government bodies, the defining feature of G2G." },
  { id: "gov-46", topic: "eg-models", q: "A well-designed G2B tax-filing portal primarily aims to reduce which burden for businesses?", options: ["The burden of hiring more staff to physically visit multiple government offices, submit paper forms, and manually track compliance status", "The requirement that businesses pay any taxes at all", "The need for any digital connectivity whatsoever", "The requirement that businesses maintain any financial records"], answer: 0, exp: "Digitizing G2B interactions like tax filing primarily aims to reduce administrative burden and friction, not change underlying legal/tax obligations themselves." },
  { id: "gov-47", topic: "eg-models", q: "Which statement best captures the relationship between 'Digital India' as a national program and 'India Stack' as a set of technical building blocks?", options: ["They are entirely unrelated, with India Stack having no connection to Digital India", "India Stack represents a key set of technical digital public infrastructure components (identity, payments, data) that support and enable many of Digital India's broader service-delivery and empowerment goals", "Digital India is a private company's product, unrelated to any government initiative", "India Stack replaced Digital India entirely as of a specific date"], answer: 1, exp: "India Stack's open APIs are widely cited as foundational technical infrastructure enabling many Digital India service-delivery outcomes." },
  { id: "gov-48", topic: "eg-models", q: "A key success factor for any collaborative digital platform aiming to serve citizens at scale (like UMANG) is:", options: ["Ensuring it works only on the newest, most expensive smartphones", "Reliability, broad device/network compatibility, and genuine usability for citizens with varying levels of digital literacy and connectivity", "Requiring every user to have a high-speed broadband connection with no lower-bandwidth option", "Limiting the platform to a single narrow demographic group"], answer: 1, exp: "Given India's diverse population and connectivity conditions, broad accessibility and reliability are essential for a citizen-facing platform to actually achieve its intended reach." },
  { id: "gov-49", topic: "eg-models", q: "Which best explains why understanding the C2C, C2B, C2G, B2C, and G2G interaction models matters for someone working in IT/e-governance?", options: ["These models have no practical relevance to designing or evaluating digital systems", "Recognizing which interaction model a given digital service falls under helps clarify its purpose, stakeholders, and the specific design/security/policy considerations that are most relevant to it", "Only one single model is ever relevant in any real-world project", "These models apply exclusively to countries other than India"], answer: 1, exp: "Correctly identifying the interaction model helps frame the right requirements, stakeholders, and risks for a given digital governance initiative." },
  { id: "gov-50", topic: "eg-models", q: "Overall, the roles of Commerce, Business, and Governance in Digital India, along with the various interaction models (G2G, C2C, C2B, C2G, B2C, and others) and collaborative APIs/platforms, together illustrate:", options: ["A single, monolithic, isolated government IT system with no external interaction", "A broad, interconnected digital ecosystem in which government, businesses, and citizens increasingly interact, transact, and collaborate through shared, interoperable digital infrastructure", "A concept with no practical relevance to real-world government digital transformation", "An approach that deliberately excludes any private-sector or citizen participation"], answer: 1, exp: "This interconnected, multi-stakeholder digital ecosystem view is central to how modern e-governance strategies like Digital India are designed." },

  // ================= Enterprise Systems, EDI/ERP & IT Governance (50) =================
  { id: "ent-01", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "EDI (Electronic Data Interchange) is best defined as:", options: ["A social media messaging protocol", "The structured, standardized electronic exchange of business documents (like purchase orders, invoices) directly between organizations' computer systems, without manual re-entry", "A type of physical mail delivery service", "A programming language used only for web development"], answer: 1, exp: "EDI replaces manual, paper-based document exchange with direct, structured, computer-to-computer data transfer." },
  { id: "ent-02", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "A key benefit of EDI over traditional paper-based business document exchange is:", options: ["Slower processing and higher error rates", "Faster processing, reduced manual data-entry errors, and lower transaction costs between trading partners", "The complete elimination of any need for data validation", "Requiring more paper and physical storage"], answer: 1, exp: "Automating document exchange directly between systems reduces both processing time and the errors inherent in manual re-entry." },
  { id: "ent-03", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "For EDI to work effectively between two different organizations' systems, they typically need to agree on:", options: ["Nothing; any format works interchangeably with no standardization", "A common data format/standard (such as ANSI X12 or EDIFACT) so that both systems can correctly interpret the exchanged documents", "Using only handwritten documents scanned as images", "Sharing a single physical office location"], answer: 1, exp: "Standardized formats are essential so that trading partners' different systems can reliably interpret exchanged documents." },
  { id: "ent-04", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "ERP (Enterprise Resource Planning) systems are best described as:", options: ["A single-purpose tool that only manages email", "Integrated software suites that manage and coordinate an organization's core business processes (finance, HR, supply chain, manufacturing, etc.) within a unified system", "A type of external EDI-only messaging network", "A tool used exclusively for graphic design"], answer: 1, exp: "ERP's defining trait is integration — bringing together previously siloed business functions into one coordinated system." },
  { id: "ent-05", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "A key benefit of an ERP system, compared to using separate, disconnected departmental software tools, is:", options: ["Increased data duplication and inconsistency across departments", "A single, shared source of consistent data across departments, improving coordination, reporting accuracy, and operational efficiency", "Making cross-department reporting impossible", "Requiring each department to maintain entirely separate, unrelated databases"], answer: 1, exp: "ERP's central data model reduces the inconsistency and duplication that arise from disconnected departmental systems." },
  { id: "ent-06", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "Common functional modules found in a typical ERP system include:", options: ["Only a single module handling advertising", "Finance/accounting, human resources, supply chain/inventory management, and manufacturing/production planning, among others", "Only modules related to physical building security", "Only a social media management module"], answer: 1, exp: "ERP systems are modular, typically covering the major functional areas of an organization's operations." },
  { id: "ent-07", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "Citizen Relationship Management (CzRM), applying CRM concepts to government, primarily focuses on:", options: ["Managing a private company's sales pipeline with no government relevance", "Systematically tracking and managing citizens' interactions, service requests, and grievances with government to improve service quality and responsiveness", "Only managing government employees' internal HR records", "A synonym for ERP with no distinction"], answer: 1, exp: "CzRM adapts private-sector customer relationship management principles to the government-citizen relationship, emphasizing service quality and responsiveness." },
  { id: "ent-08", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "A well-implemented Citizen Relationship Management system helps government agencies primarily by:", options: ["Making it harder to track whether citizen complaints are resolved", "Providing visibility into citizen service requests/complaints across channels, enabling better tracking, faster resolution, and data-driven service improvement", "Eliminating the need for any citizen feedback mechanism", "Only benefiting a single government department with no cross-agency value"], answer: 1, exp: "Centralized tracking of citizen interactions supports more responsive, accountable, and continuously improving public service delivery." },
  { id: "ent-09", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "An organization's IT/ITeS usage policy is intended to:", options: ["Only describe the organization's marketing strategy", "Define acceptable use, security expectations, and governance rules for how employees and systems use IT/IT-enabled services within the organization", "Replace the need for any actual IT systems", "Only apply to organizations with fewer than five employees"], answer: 1, exp: "A usage policy establishes clear expectations and rules governing how IT resources are appropriately used within an organization." },
  { id: "ent-10", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "When devising an IT/ITeS usage policy for enterprise applications, a key consideration is:", options: ["Ignoring any alignment with the organization's broader business objectives and risk tolerance", "Balancing security, compliance, and operational needs with usability, ensuring the policy is practical and aligned with the organization's specific context and risk profile", "Writing a policy so restrictive that no employee could ever actually use any IT system", "Copying another organization's policy verbatim with no adaptation to context"], answer: 1, exp: "Effective policies are tailored to an organization's actual needs, risks, and operating context, rather than being generic or impractically restrictive." },
  { id: "ent-11", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "COBIT5 (Control Objectives for Information and Related Technologies) is best described as:", options: ["A specific software product sold by a single vendor", "A widely recognized framework for the governance and management of enterprise IT, helping align IT activities with business goals and manage IT-related risk", "A programming language for building enterprise applications", "A type of hardware networking cable"], answer: 1, exp: "COBIT5 is a governance/management framework, not a specific product, providing structured guidance for enterprise IT governance." },
  { id: "ent-12", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "Among COBIT5's stated core principles is:", options: ["Ignoring stakeholder needs entirely", "Meeting stakeholder needs — ensuring enterprise IT governance and management create value by balancing benefits, risk, and resource use for stakeholders", "Only focusing on a single isolated IT department with no enterprise-wide view", "Treating governance and management as identical, interchangeable activities"], answer: 1, exp: "This is one of COBIT5's five core principles, emphasizing that IT governance should ultimately serve stakeholder value." },
  { id: "ent-13", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "Another core COBIT5 principle is 'covering the enterprise end-to-end', which means:", options: ["The framework should only apply narrowly to the IT department, ignoring the rest of the organization", "IT governance should be integrated with overall enterprise governance, treating IT-related governance and management as part of the whole organization rather than an isolated silo", "Only the CEO should ever be involved in IT decisions", "The framework applies only to very large multinational organizations"], answer: 1, exp: "COBIT5 explicitly rejects treating IT governance as a siloed, IT-only concern, instead integrating it with broader enterprise governance." },
  { id: "ent-14", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "COBIT5's principle of 'applying a single, integrated framework' means:", options: ["Organizations should adopt as many disconnected, conflicting frameworks as possible", "COBIT5 aligns with and integrates other relevant standards/frameworks, providing one comprehensive, high-level framework rather than requiring organizations to reconcile many disconnected ones", "The framework only works in complete isolation from any other IT standard", "Integration is explicitly discouraged under COBIT5"], answer: 1, exp: "COBIT5 was designed to integrate with and reference other major frameworks/standards, aiming to be a single overarching framework." },
  { id: "ent-15", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "COBIT5's principle of 'enabling a holistic approach' emphasizes that effective IT governance requires considering multiple interacting factors, such as:", options: ["Only the technology itself, with no consideration of people or processes", "Processes, organizational structures, culture/ethics, information, people/skills, and services/infrastructure, all working together", "Only budget considerations, with no attention to people or process", "A single narrow technical control with no broader context"], answer: 1, exp: "COBIT5 recognizes that governance is not just about technology — it requires coordinated attention across multiple interacting enabler categories." },
  { id: "ent-16", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "COBIT5's principle of 'separating governance from management' distinguishes between:", options: ["Two identical activities with no meaningful distinction", "Governance (setting direction, evaluating options, monitoring performance — typically a board-level responsibility) and management (planning, building, running, and monitoring activities in line with that direction — typically an executive-level responsibility)", "Only IT staff performing governance, with no role in management ever", "A distinction that applies only to non-IT parts of an organization"], answer: 1, exp: "This governance-vs-management distinction is a foundational COBIT5 concept, clarifying different levels of accountability within an organization." },
  { id: "ent-17", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "A key reason organizations adopt frameworks like COBIT5 for IT governance is to:", options: ["Guarantee that no IT-related risk or failure will ever occur", "Provide a structured, widely recognized approach for aligning IT investment/activity with business objectives while managing associated risks", "Replace the need for any actual IT staff or systems", "Only satisfy a legal requirement with no practical operational benefit"], answer: 1, exp: "COBIT5 and similar frameworks provide structure and best-practice guidance, though they don't eliminate risk entirely — they help manage it more effectively." },
  { id: "ent-18", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "In an EDI transaction, a 'trading partner' refers to:", options: ["A random, unrelated third party with no business relationship", "The other organization with which a business exchanges structured electronic documents (such as a supplier or customer)", "Only a government regulatory agency", "A type of software bug"], answer: 1, exp: "EDI is inherently a bilateral (or multilateral) arrangement between organizations that have agreed to exchange structured documents electronically." },
  { id: "ent-19", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "EDI is most commonly used for business documents such as:", options: ["Only personal social media posts", "Purchase orders, invoices, shipping notices, and similar recurring, structured business transactions between organizations", "Only marketing brochures with no structured data", "Only internal employee performance reviews"], answer: 1, exp: "EDI is well suited to high-volume, structured, repetitive business documents exchanged between trading partners." },
  { id: "ent-20", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "ERP systems typically provide organizations with which key operational benefit?", options: ["Increased manual data re-entry across departments", "Real-time (or near real-time) visibility into business operations across departments, supporting better-informed decision-making", "Complete isolation of each department's data from every other department", "Elimination of the need for any reporting or analytics"], answer: 1, exp: "Integrated, real-time visibility across departments is one of ERP's central value propositions for organizational decision-making." },
  { id: "ent-21", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "A common challenge organizations face when implementing an ERP system is:", options: ["ERP implementations are always trivially easy with no organizational impact", "Significant complexity, cost, and organizational change management required to successfully integrate and adopt the system across departments", "ERP systems require no configuration or customization whatsoever", "ERP implementation never requires any employee training"], answer: 1, exp: "ERP implementations are widely recognized as complex, resource-intensive projects requiring careful change management, not simple plug-and-play deployments." },
  { id: "ent-22", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "For public sector/enterprise applications, a well-devised IT usage policy typically addresses:", options: ["Only which font should be used in documents, with no security relevance", "Acceptable use of systems/data, security responsibilities, data handling/privacy requirements, and consequences for policy violations", "Only the organization's holiday calendar", "Only the physical layout of office furniture"], answer: 1, exp: "A comprehensive IT usage policy needs to cover both what is permitted and the security/privacy responsibilities that come with system access." },
  { id: "ent-23", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "Which best describes the relationship between EDI and ERP in a typical enterprise IT landscape?", options: ["They are identical, interchangeable technologies with no distinction", "EDI focuses on structured electronic document exchange BETWEEN organizations, while ERP focuses on integrating processes and data WITHIN a single organization — the two are often integrated together (e.g., an ERP system automatically generating/receiving EDI documents)", "EDI and ERP have no possible connection or integration", "ERP is only used for personal, non-business purposes"], answer: 1, exp: "EDI (inter-organizational document exchange) and ERP (intra-organizational process integration) are complementary and are frequently integrated in practice." },
  { id: "ent-24", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "Citizen Relationship Management systems, when applied to a smart-city or e-governance context, are often integrated with:", options: ["No other systems whatsoever, operating in complete isolation", "Citizen grievance/complaint portals, service request tracking, and sometimes broader smart-city data platforms, to provide a coordinated view of citizen interactions", "Only a single department's internal payroll system", "Only physical, paper-based complaint registers with no digital component"], answer: 1, exp: "CzRM systems are most valuable when integrated with the broader digital service-delivery ecosystem, giving a coordinated picture of citizen engagement." },
  { id: "ent-25", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "A government agency devising an IT/ITeS usage policy for its enterprise applications should generally align that policy with:", options: ["No external standards or frameworks whatsoever", "Relevant IT governance frameworks (such as COBIT5), applicable laws/regulations, and the organization's own risk and operational context", "A policy copied word-for-word from an unrelated foreign private company with no adaptation", "Only the personal preferences of a single IT staff member, with no broader organizational input"], answer: 1, exp: "Good policy development draws on recognized frameworks and applicable regulations while remaining tailored to the specific organization's context." },
  { id: "ent-26", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "Which of these would be a typical example of an EDI transaction between a retailer and its supplier?", options: ["A retailer's employee handwriting a purchase order and mailing it via postal service", "The retailer's system automatically generating and electronically transmitting a structured purchase order directly into the supplier's system", "A phone call placing a verbal order with no written record", "An in-person meeting to discuss a purchase with no data exchange"], answer: 1, exp: "This automated, structured, system-to-system transmission is the defining characteristic of an EDI transaction." },
  { id: "ent-27", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "A key security consideration when implementing EDI between organizations is:", options: ["No security is needed since EDI only ever involves trusted internal systems", "Ensuring the confidentiality, integrity, and authentication of exchanged documents, since sensitive business data is flowing directly between separate organizations' systems", "Making EDI documents publicly accessible to anyone on the internet", "EDI systems require no authentication of trading partners whatsoever"], answer: 1, exp: "Because EDI involves exchanging potentially sensitive business data across organizational boundaries, security controls are essential." },
  { id: "ent-28", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "A key reason large organizations (including government agencies) invest in ERP systems despite their complexity/cost is:", options: ["ERP systems provide no meaningful operational benefit to justify their cost", "The long-term benefits of integrated data, streamlined processes, and improved decision-making generally outweigh the significant upfront implementation investment for organizations of sufficient scale/complexity", "ERP systems are required by law in every single country with no exception", "ERP systems eliminate the need for any human staff"], answer: 1, exp: "For sufficiently large, complex organizations, the operational benefits of integration typically justify the substantial implementation investment." },
  { id: "ent-29", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "COBIT5's distinction between 'governance' (direction-setting/oversight) and 'management' (execution) is important for organizations because:", options: ["The distinction has no practical value and can be safely ignored", "Blurring these roles can lead to poor accountability — governance bodies (like a board) need to set direction and monitor without micromanaging, while management needs clear direction to execute effectively", "Only management matters; governance is entirely unnecessary", "Only governance matters; management is entirely unnecessary"], answer: 1, exp: "Clear separation of governance and management roles supports better accountability and more effective organizational decision-making." },
  { id: "ent-30", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "Applying IT governance principles (like those in COBIT5) to a government's enterprise applications helps ensure:", options: ["IT investments and risk management are disconnected from the government's actual public service objectives", "IT investments, risk management, and resource use are aligned with and support the organization's broader public service goals and accountability requirements", "Governance frameworks apply only to private companies, never public sector organizations", "There is no need for any oversight of government IT spending or performance"], answer: 1, exp: "IT governance frameworks help ensure that government IT investment genuinely serves public objectives and is subject to appropriate oversight and accountability." },
  { id: "ent-31", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "Which of these best illustrates the value of integrating a Citizen Relationship Management system with backend departmental systems?", options: ["A citizen's complaint is logged but then completely lost with no connection to the department responsible for resolving it", "A citizen's complaint filed through a central portal is automatically routed to, and tracked within, the specific department responsible, with status visible to the citizen throughout", "Integration serves no practical purpose and should be avoided", "Citizens should never be able to track the status of their own complaints"], answer: 1, exp: "This integrated routing and tracking is exactly the kind of coordinated, transparent citizen experience CzRM systems aim to enable." },
  { id: "ent-32", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "A well-governed enterprise application landscape (informed by frameworks like COBIT5) typically includes clear accountability for:", options: ["No one; accountability should be deliberately left undefined", "Who owns which systems/processes, who is accountable for risk and compliance, and how IT investment decisions are made and reviewed", "Only the most junior IT staff member, with no senior oversight", "Only external vendors, with no internal accountability whatsoever"], answer: 1, exp: "Clear accountability structures are a hallmark of mature IT governance, helping ensure decisions and risks are properly owned and managed." },
  { id: "ent-33", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "EDI's structured document standards (like ANSI X12 or EDIFACT) primarily solve which problem?", options: ["They solve no real problem and add unnecessary complexity", "Without a shared, agreed-upon format, two organizations' different computer systems would not be able to reliably interpret each other's exchanged business documents", "They are only relevant for documents printed on paper", "They eliminate the need for any data at all in a business transaction"], answer: 1, exp: "Standardization is what makes automated, system-to-system document exchange between different organizations' systems actually workable." },
  { id: "ent-34", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "Which best describes how ERP systems typically handle data across an organization's different functional areas (finance, HR, supply chain, etc.)?", options: ["Each functional area maintains a completely separate, disconnected copy of its own data with no sharing", "ERP systems generally maintain a shared, centralized (or tightly integrated) data model, so data entered in one module is consistently available to relevant other modules", "ERP systems have no concept of data at all", "Data in an ERP system can only ever be viewed by a single specific employee"], answer: 1, exp: "This shared, integrated data model is precisely what distinguishes ERP from a collection of disconnected departmental tools." },
  { id: "ent-35", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "A key IT/ITeS usage policy consideration for organizations increasingly relying on cloud-based enterprise applications is:", options: ["Cloud usage requires no policy consideration whatsoever", "Ensuring the policy addresses appropriate data handling, security, and vendor/third-party risk management specific to cloud-hosted services", "Policies should only ever address on-premises, physically hosted systems", "Cloud-based applications eliminate the need for any usage policy"], answer: 1, exp: "As enterprise applications increasingly move to the cloud, usage policies need to explicitly address the distinct risks and responsibilities that model introduces." },
  { id: "ent-36", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "Which scenario best illustrates COBIT5's 'meeting stakeholder needs' principle in practice?", options: ["An IT department pursuing a large technology investment with no consideration of whether it delivers value to the organization's actual stakeholders", "An organization evaluating a proposed IT investment specifically in terms of the benefits, risks, and resource costs it will create for the organization's stakeholders, before proceeding", "Ignoring stakeholder input entirely when making IT investment decisions", "Only considering the preferences of a single external vendor, with no attention to internal stakeholders"], answer: 1, exp: "This benefits/risk/resource evaluation, explicitly framed around stakeholder value, is exactly what the 'meeting stakeholder needs' principle calls for." },
  { id: "ent-37", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "A key difference between how a small organization and a large, complex organization (like a national government) might apply COBIT5 is:", options: ["COBIT5 can only ever be applied by very small organizations", "COBIT5 is designed to be scalable/adaptable — larger, more complex organizations may apply its principles and enablers more extensively or formally than a smaller one, but the underlying principles remain broadly applicable", "COBIT5 has no relevance whatsoever to large, complex organizations", "COBIT5 requires an identical, rigid implementation regardless of organizational size or complexity"], answer: 1, exp: "IT governance frameworks like COBIT5 are generally designed to scale in their application depth based on organizational size and complexity." },
  { id: "ent-38", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "A government enterprise application handling citizen data should generally have an IT/ITeS usage policy that explicitly addresses:", options: ["Nothing related to data privacy or citizen data protection", "Appropriate access controls, data privacy protections, and compliance with applicable data protection regulations, given the sensitivity of citizen data", "Only which employees are allowed to take lunch breaks", "Only the office's internet bandwidth allocation, with no data-handling guidance"], answer: 1, exp: "Given the sensitivity of citizen data, usage policies for government enterprise applications must explicitly address privacy and data protection." },
  { id: "ent-39", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "Which best describes the overall goal of combining EDI, ERP, and citizen relationship management approaches within a government's digital transformation strategy?", options: ["To keep every system completely disconnected from every other system", "To streamline both inter-organizational transactions (EDI), internal process integration (ERP), and citizen-facing service management (CzRM) as complementary pieces of a coherent, efficient digital government/enterprise ecosystem", "To eliminate the need for any citizen interaction with government entirely", "To ensure government systems have no connection whatsoever to private-sector business processes"], answer: 1, exp: "These three capabilities address different but complementary layers of an organization's/government's digital operations, working together rather than in isolation." },
  { id: "ent-40", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "Which of these is a realistic driver for a government agency to adopt ERP-style integrated systems internally?", options: ["A desire to make cross-department reporting and coordination more difficult", "A need to improve budget tracking, resource allocation, and cross-department coordination through consistent, integrated data rather than disconnected departmental spreadsheets/systems", "ERP systems have no relevance to government operations, only private business", "A legal requirement that government systems must never share any data"], answer: 1, exp: "Government agencies face many of the same internal coordination and data-consistency challenges that drive private-sector ERP adoption." },
  { id: "ent-41", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "A well-governed IT/ITeS usage policy should typically be:", options: ["Written once and never reviewed or updated again", "Periodically reviewed and updated to remain aligned with evolving technology, threats, regulations, and organizational needs", "Kept secret from the employees who are actually expected to follow it", "Applied inconsistently, with different rules for different employees with no clear justification"], answer: 1, exp: "Given how quickly technology and threats evolve, usage policies need periodic review to remain relevant and effective." },
  { id: "ent-42", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "Which best explains why 'holistic' thinking (as emphasized by COBIT5) matters when designing enterprise IT governance for a large organization?", options: ["Technology alone, considered in isolation from people and process, is always sufficient to ensure successful IT governance", "Effective governance requires coordinated attention to interacting factors — technology, people, process, culture, and information — since focusing on only one factor in isolation tends to produce incomplete or ineffective outcomes", "Holistic thinking is an unnecessary complication with no practical benefit", "Only the organization's budget matters; all other factors are irrelevant"], answer: 1, exp: "COBIT5's holistic 'enablers' concept reflects the reality that IT governance failures often stem from neglecting non-technical factors like people, culture, or process." },
  { id: "ent-43", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "A retailer's ERP system automatically triggering an EDI purchase order to a supplier when inventory falls below a threshold illustrates:", options: ["Two completely unrelated, non-integrated technologies with no practical connection", "A practical integration of internal process automation (ERP) with inter-organizational document exchange (EDI), streamlining the reordering process end-to-end", "A manual, entirely paper-based process with no automation involved", "A process that could never realistically occur in practice"], answer: 1, exp: "This kind of ERP-EDI integration, automating reordering based on internal inventory triggers, is a common real-world enterprise automation pattern." },
  { id: "ent-44", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "Which of the following would most likely fall under the scope of an organization's IT/ITeS usage policy?", options: ["The organization's overall marketing slogan", "Rules on acceptable use of email/internet, password requirements, data classification/handling, and consequences for security violations", "The physical color scheme chosen for the office walls", "The organization's annual holiday party planning"], answer: 1, exp: "These are exactly the kinds of practical, IT-related rules and expectations a usage policy is meant to define." },
  { id: "ent-45", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "A government agency seeking COBIT5-aligned IT governance maturity would likely focus on improving:", options: ["Only the physical appearance of its data center, with no attention to processes or accountability", "Clear governance structures, defined processes, risk management practices, and measurable performance/value delivered by its IT investments", "Eliminating all documentation of IT processes and decisions", "Reducing all forms of IT-related oversight and accountability"], answer: 1, exp: "COBIT5-style maturity generally involves strengthening structured processes, accountability, and measurable performance across IT governance activities." },
  { id: "ent-46", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "Which best describes why 'separating governance from management' (a COBIT5 principle) is particularly relevant to public sector IT initiatives?", options: ["Public sector organizations have no need for any governance oversight of IT", "It helps ensure elected/appointed leadership can set clear strategic direction and hold executive management accountable for delivering it, rather than leadership becoming entangled in day-to-day operational execution", "Public sector IT should be managed identically to how a small private startup manages its systems, with no distinction", "This principle applies only to fully private, for-profit organizations"], answer: 1, exp: "Clear governance/management separation supports the kind of accountability structures expected in public sector oversight of IT initiatives and spending." },
  { id: "ent-47", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "Which of these is a realistic example of how a Citizen Relationship Management approach could support 'services on demand' goals under Digital India?", options: ["Citizens have no way to track the status of any service request they've submitted", "A citizen can submit a service request online, track its status in real time, and receive automated updates as it moves through the responsible department's process", "CzRM systems are entirely unrelated to the concept of services on demand", "Citizens must physically visit an office weekly to manually check on the status of their request"], answer: 1, exp: "This kind of transparent, trackable, self-service experience is exactly what CzRM-supported 'services on demand' aims to deliver." },
  { id: "ent-48", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "Which best summarizes the practical relationship between EDI, ERP, and IT governance frameworks like COBIT5?", options: ["They are entirely unrelated concepts that never appear together in practice", "EDI and ERP are examples of enterprise technology/process capabilities, while COBIT5 provides an overarching governance framework that can help ensure such technology investments and processes are well-managed, aligned with objectives, and appropriately governed", "COBIT5 replaces the need for any actual ERP or EDI systems", "EDI and ERP make IT governance frameworks like COBIT5 completely unnecessary"], answer: 1, exp: "Governance frameworks like COBIT5 provide the overarching structure within which specific technology capabilities like ERP and EDI are planned, managed, and governed." },
  { id: "ent-49", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "A key long-term benefit of investing in well-governed enterprise systems (ERP, EDI, CzRM) supported by frameworks like COBIT5 is:", options: ["Guaranteed elimination of all future IT-related risk or failure", "More consistent, efficient, and accountable operations, better decision-making from integrated data, and improved service delivery to citizens/customers — while risk is managed, not eliminated entirely", "No measurable improvement in organizational performance whatsoever", "Complete independence from any need for skilled IT staff"], answer: 1, exp: "These structured investments improve — but don't eliminate risk from — an organization's overall efficiency, accountability, and service quality." },
  { id: "ent-50", topic: "eg-enterprise", q: "Overall, EDI, ERP, Citizen Relationship Management, IT/ITeS usage policy, and frameworks like COBIT5 together represent:", options: ["A random, disconnected collection of unrelated enterprise IT concepts", "Complementary building blocks for managing an organization's (including a government's) internal processes, inter-organizational transactions, citizen/customer relationships, and overall IT governance in a structured, accountable, efficient way", "Concepts relevant only to purely private-sector, for-profit organizations", "Tools that eliminate the need for any human judgment or oversight in enterprise IT management"], answer: 1, exp: "Together, these concepts span internal integration (ERP), external exchange (EDI), citizen/customer engagement (CzRM), operational rules (usage policy), and overarching governance (COBIT5) — complementary layers of enterprise IT management." },

  // ================= Smart Villages, Smart Cities & Citizen Empowerment (50) =================
  { id: "smc-01", topic: "eg-smart", q: "A 'Smart City', as an urban development concept, is best described as:", options: ["A city with no use of technology whatsoever", "An urban area that leverages information and communication technology (including IoT/sensors and data) to improve infrastructure, services, sustainability, and overall quality of life for residents", "A city defined purely by its physical size or population", "A city that exists only in fictional or theoretical contexts"], answer: 1, exp: "Smart cities use technology and data as tools to improve how urban services and infrastructure function for residents." },
  { id: "smc-02", topic: "eg-smart", q: "India's Smart Cities Mission, a government initiative, primarily aims to:", options: ["Ban the use of technology in Indian cities", "Develop selected cities with core infrastructure, a clean and sustainable environment, and application of 'smart' technology-driven solutions to improve quality of life", "Only benefit a single private technology vendor", "Focus exclusively on rural areas with no urban component"], answer: 1, exp: "The Smart Cities Mission is a flagship urban development program applying technology and better planning to improve city infrastructure and services." },
  { id: "smc-03", topic: "eg-smart", q: "Typical technology-enabled components of a smart city include:", options: ["Only decorative city monuments with no functional technology", "Smart traffic management, intelligent utility (water/electricity) monitoring, e-governance service delivery, and integrated urban data/command centers", "Only physical road construction with no data or sensor component", "A complete absence of any citizen-facing digital services"], answer: 1, exp: "Smart city initiatives typically combine sensor/IoT data, integrated management systems, and citizen-facing digital services." },
  { id: "smc-04", topic: "eg-smart", q: "A 'Smart Village' extends the smart city concept to rural areas by focusing on:", options: ["Removing all existing rural infrastructure with no replacement", "Improving rural connectivity, access to digital services, agricultural technology, and essential utilities through appropriate, context-suited technology solutions", "Only applying identical solutions used in large cities with no adaptation to rural needs", "Excluding rural populations from any digital initiative entirely"], answer: 1, exp: "Smart village initiatives adapt technology-driven improvement to the specific needs and constraints of rural communities, rather than simply copying urban solutions." },
  { id: "smc-05", topic: "eg-smart", q: "A key challenge in extending 'smart' digital initiatives to rural/village areas, compared to urban centers, is:", options: ["Rural areas always have identical infrastructure and connectivity to major cities", "Rural areas often face greater challenges with connectivity infrastructure, digital literacy, and lower population density, requiring tailored approaches rather than a direct copy of urban smart-city solutions", "There is no meaningful difference in challenges between rural and urban digital initiatives", "Rural areas have no need for any digital services whatsoever"], answer: 1, exp: "Connectivity gaps and lower population density are real practical challenges that shape how smart village solutions must be designed differently from urban ones." },
  { id: "smc-06", topic: "eg-smart", q: "'e-Utilities to citizens' refers to:", options: ["Physical utility poles and wiring with no digital component", "The delivery of utility services (like electricity billing, water supply information, or utility complaint management) to citizens through electronic/digital channels", "Only in-person utility bill payment at a physical counter", "A term unrelated to actual utility services"], answer: 1, exp: "Digitizing utility service delivery (bill payment, outage reporting, usage monitoring) improves convenience and efficiency for both citizens and utility providers." },
  { id: "smc-07", topic: "eg-smart", q: "'Services on demand', as a smart-city/e-governance principle, emphasizes:", options: ["Citizens must wait indefinitely with no defined service timeline", "Government/utility services being available to citizens conveniently, often in real time or with predictable turnaround, rather than requiring lengthy in-person bureaucratic processes", "Eliminating all government services entirely", "Only offering services once per year on a fixed schedule"], answer: 1, exp: "This principle reflects a shift toward citizen-centric, timely, and convenient service delivery, a core aim of both Digital India and smart city initiatives." },
  { id: "smc-08", topic: "eg-smart", q: "'e-Empowerment', in the context of Digital India/smart cities, refers to:", options: ["Restricting citizens' access to information and digital tools", "Enabling citizens through digital access, information, skills, and participation opportunities to make more informed decisions and better exercise their rights/opportunities", "A purely theoretical concept with no practical application", "Only benefiting government employees, never ordinary citizens"], answer: 1, exp: "e-Empowerment focuses on genuinely enabling citizens — through access, literacy, and participation — not just providing technology without meaningful benefit." },
  { id: "smc-09", topic: "eg-smart", q: "'e-Employment' initiatives typically involve:", options: ["Eliminating all traditional job-seeking processes with no digital alternative provided", "Online job portals, digital skill-development platforms, and digital employment exchanges that connect job seekers with opportunities and training", "Only benefiting large multinational corporations, with no relevance to individual job seekers", "A term with no practical real-world application"], answer: 1, exp: "e-Employment initiatives (like India's National Career Service portal) use digital platforms to connect job seekers with opportunities and skill development resources." },
  { id: "smc-10", topic: "eg-smart", q: "An integrated urban 'command and control center', found in many smart city implementations, primarily serves to:", options: ["Only display decorative screens with no operational function", "Aggregate real-time data from various city systems (traffic, utilities, safety, etc.) into a centralized view, supporting coordinated monitoring and rapid response by city administrators", "Replace the need for any actual city government or administration", "Only be used once per year for ceremonial purposes"], answer: 1, exp: "These centers give city administrators real-time, integrated situational awareness across multiple urban systems, supporting more effective coordination and response." },
  { id: "smc-11", topic: "eg-smart", q: "Smart traffic management systems, a common smart-city component, typically use technology to:", options: ["Increase traffic congestion deliberately", "Monitor and optimize traffic flow in real time (e.g., adaptive traffic signals, congestion monitoring), improving efficiency and reducing delays", "Eliminate the need for any roads or vehicles", "Only function during a single specific hour of the day"], answer: 1, exp: "Real-time traffic monitoring and adaptive signal control are widely deployed smart-city applications aimed at improving urban mobility." },
  { id: "smc-12", topic: "eg-smart", q: "Which best illustrates a practical example of 'e-Utilities to citizens' in a smart city?", options: ["A citizen must physically visit the electricity board office monthly with no online alternative", "A citizen can view their real-time electricity usage, pay their bill online, and report an outage through a mobile app", "Utility services are provided with no way for citizens to interact with the provider at all", "Utility bills can only be paid using a specific outdated payment method with no digital option"], answer: 1, exp: "This kind of convenient, digital self-service utility interaction is exactly what 'e-Utilities to citizens' initiatives aim to provide." },
  { id: "smc-13", topic: "eg-smart", q: "A key benefit of 'services on demand' for citizens, as opposed to traditional fixed-hours, in-person-only government service delivery, is:", options: ["Reduced convenience and accessibility for citizens", "Greater flexibility, faster turnaround, and reduced dependency on physically visiting a government office during limited hours", "No meaningful benefit compared to traditional service delivery", "Services becoming available only to a small, privileged subset of citizens"], answer: 1, exp: "Digital, on-demand service delivery removes many of the time/location constraints inherent in traditional in-person-only government services." },
  { id: "smc-14", topic: "eg-smart", q: "Digital skill-development platforms, supporting e-Employment goals, primarily aim to:", options: ["Discourage citizens from acquiring any new skills", "Help citizens acquire relevant skills (often digital or vocational) to improve their employability in a changing economy", "Only be accessible to those who are already highly skilled, with no benefit for beginners", "Replace the need for any formal education system entirely"], answer: 1, exp: "Skill-development platforms aim to broaden access to relevant training, supporting better employment outcomes, particularly for underserved populations." },
  { id: "smc-15", topic: "eg-smart", q: "A smart city's use of IoT (Internet of Things) sensors (e.g., for air quality, water levels, or waste bin fill levels) primarily supports:", options: ["No practical function; sensors are purely decorative", "Real-time monitoring and data-driven decision-making for city services, enabling more proactive and efficient management of urban infrastructure", "Replacing the need for any human city administrators entirely", "Only functioning in laboratory settings, never real urban deployments"], answer: 1, exp: "IoT sensor data feeds directly into the kind of data-driven, proactive management that defines many smart-city applications." },
  { id: "smc-16", topic: "eg-smart", q: "A key equity consideration for smart city and smart village initiatives is:", options: ["Ensuring benefits are concentrated only among the wealthiest, most digitally literate residents", "Designing initiatives so that benefits reach a broad population, including those with lower digital literacy, limited connectivity, or economic disadvantage, rather than deepening existing inequalities", "Equity has no relevance to smart city/village planning", "Only urban, never rural, populations should ever be considered"], answer: 1, exp: "Without deliberate attention to equity, technology-driven initiatives risk primarily benefiting already-advantaged populations while leaving others further behind." },
  { id: "smc-17", topic: "eg-smart", q: "Which best describes how smart village initiatives might differ practically from smart city initiatives in their choice of technology solutions?", options: ["They should always use the exact same technology solutions with no adaptation whatsoever", "Smart village solutions often need to account for lower population density, different connectivity infrastructure, and agriculture-related needs, whereas smart city solutions may focus more on dense urban infrastructure like traffic and high-rise utility management", "Smart villages should have no technology component at all", "There is no meaningful distinction between the two contexts"], answer: 1, exp: "Effective smart village solutions are tailored to rural context and needs (e.g., agri-tech, connectivity extension) rather than simply replicating urban smart-city approaches." },
  { id: "smc-18", topic: "eg-smart", q: "The National Career Service (NCS) portal in India is an example of:", options: ["A private company's proprietary job board with no government involvement", "A government e-employment initiative connecting job seekers, employers, and skill-development/career guidance resources through a digital platform", "A purely social media networking site with no employment relevance", "A physical, paper-based employment exchange with no digital component"], answer: 1, exp: "NCS exemplifies a government-led digital platform supporting e-Employment goals by connecting job seekers with opportunities and career services." },
  { id: "smc-19", topic: "eg-smart", q: "Which of these best illustrates 'e-Empowerment' in practice?", options: ["A citizen has no access to any information about government schemes they may be eligible for", "A citizen uses a government portal to learn about welfare schemes they qualify for, apply directly online, and track the application's progress, without needing intermediaries", "Citizens are actively discouraged from directly accessing any government information", "Only government officials, never citizens, are given access to relevant scheme information"], answer: 1, exp: "Direct, self-service access to information and services — reducing dependence on intermediaries — is a concrete expression of e-Empowerment." },
  { id: "smc-20", topic: "eg-smart", q: "A smart city's integrated data platform combining traffic, utility, and public safety data primarily supports which broader goal?", options: ["Keeping different city departments' data completely siloed and disconnected from each other", "More coordinated, efficient, and responsive city management by breaking down data silos between different urban service domains", "Preventing any data-driven decision-making by city administrators", "Only benefiting a single department with no cross-functional value"], answer: 1, exp: "Integrating previously siloed data domains is central to enabling the coordinated, cross-functional decision-making that smart city initiatives aim for." },
  { id: "smc-21", topic: "eg-smart", q: "A key infrastructure prerequisite for both smart city and smart village digital initiatives to succeed is:", options: ["No infrastructure prerequisites exist; digital services work identically with or without connectivity", "Reliable digital connectivity (broadband/mobile network access), since most smart service delivery depends on citizens and systems being able to reliably connect online", "Only physical road infrastructure, with no relevance to digital connectivity", "Digital initiatives require no electricity supply whatsoever"], answer: 1, exp: "Reliable connectivity is foundational — without it, digital service delivery to citizens simply cannot function, regardless of how well-designed the services themselves are." },
  { id: "smc-22", topic: "eg-smart", q: "Which of these would be classified primarily as an 'e-Employment' initiative rather than an 'e-Utility' initiative?", options: ["An online portal for reporting a broken streetlight", "A digital platform matching job seekers with employers and providing skill-training resources", "An app for paying a monthly water bill", "A portal for tracking the status of an electricity connection application"], answer: 1, exp: "This directly addresses employment/job-matching and skills, distinguishing it from the utility-service examples in the other options." },
  { id: "smc-23", topic: "eg-smart", q: "A well-designed 'services on demand' digital government portal should generally aim to:", options: ["Require citizens to know the internal organizational structure of government before they can find the service they need", "Organize services around citizens' actual needs/life events (e.g., 'starting a business', 'renewing a license') rather than requiring citizens to first understand internal government department boundaries", "Only be accessible during a narrow 2-hour daily window", "Deliberately obscure how to actually access or complete a service"], answer: 1, exp: "Citizen-centric organization (around needs, not internal bureaucratic structure) is a widely recognized best practice for effective digital service portals." },
  { id: "smc-24", topic: "eg-smart", q: "Which best explains why smart city initiatives often emphasize public safety applications (e.g., surveillance-linked emergency response systems)?", options: ["Public safety has no relevance to smart city planning", "Technology-enabled monitoring and faster, coordinated emergency response can improve citizen safety and city resilience, though such systems also raise important privacy considerations that must be carefully balanced", "Public safety technology should always be deployed with zero privacy consideration whatsoever", "Smart cities should never include any public safety component"], answer: 1, exp: "Public safety is a common smart-city application area, though it must be balanced against legitimate privacy concerns raised by surveillance-capable systems." },
  { id: "smc-25", topic: "eg-smart", q: "A citizen using a smart-city mobile app to report a pothole, which is then automatically routed to and tracked by the relevant municipal department, illustrates:", options: ["A purely theoretical scenario with no real-world smart-city relevance", "A practical citizen-facing 'services on demand' / C2G interaction supported by integrated city data/service management systems", "An example that has nothing to do with smart-city concepts", "A process that deliberately provides no feedback to the citizen who reported the issue"], answer: 1, exp: "This is a classic real-world example combining citizen-facing digital service delivery with integrated municipal service management." },
  { id: "smc-26", topic: "eg-smart", q: "In smart village initiatives, digital agriculture technology (e.g., weather/soil data platforms for farmers) primarily aims to:", options: ["Have no relevance to farmers' actual needs or decisions", "Help farmers make more informed decisions (e.g., on irrigation, planting timing, or pest management) using timely, relevant data delivered through accessible digital channels", "Replace farmers' traditional knowledge entirely with no integration or respect for existing practices", "Only be usable by farmers with advanced computer science degrees"], answer: 1, exp: "Digital agri-tech aims to complement farmers' knowledge with timely, actionable data, improving decision-making and outcomes." },
  { id: "smc-27", topic: "eg-smart", q: "Which best describes the relationship between 'e-Empowerment' and 'digital literacy'?", options: ["They are entirely unrelated concepts with no connection", "Digital literacy (the ability to effectively use digital tools and information) is generally a necessary enabling condition for meaningful e-Empowerment — without it, citizens cannot fully benefit from available digital services and information", "Digital literacy is irrelevant as long as digital services technically exist", "e-Empowerment can be fully achieved with zero digital literacy among citizens"], answer: 1, exp: "Providing digital services alone is insufficient for empowerment if citizens lack the literacy/skills to actually use them effectively." },
  { id: "smc-28", topic: "eg-smart", q: "Which of these best illustrates a smart city 'services on demand' improvement over a traditional process?", options: ["A birth certificate that previously took weeks and multiple office visits can now be applied for and issued digitally within a much shorter, predictable timeframe", "A process that remains exactly as slow and bureaucratic as before, just described using new digital terminology", "A service that is now only available to citizens who can travel to a single central office in the capital city", "A service that has been entirely eliminated with no replacement"], answer: 0, exp: "This concrete improvement in speed, convenience, and predictability is exactly what 'services on demand' initiatives aim to achieve." },
  { id: "smc-29", topic: "eg-smart", q: "A key metric smart city planners might track to evaluate the success of a smart traffic management deployment is:", options: ["The total number of decorative traffic signal poles installed, regardless of function", "Measurable reductions in average commute time, congestion levels, or traffic incidents following deployment", "Only the cost of the technology, with no attention to any actual outcome", "A metric entirely unrelated to traffic or transportation"], answer: 1, exp: "Evaluating smart-city technology deployments against measurable outcome improvements (not just technology installation) is essential for demonstrating real value." },
  { id: "smc-30", topic: "eg-smart", q: "Which best describes why smart city/village initiatives are often described as requiring 'citizen-centric' design?", options: ["Citizen needs and usability should be entirely ignored in favor of purely technical considerations", "Ultimately, the value of these initiatives depends on whether real citizens can and do actually use and benefit from them, making usability, accessibility, and relevance to real citizen needs essential design considerations", "Citizen-centric design is a meaningless buzzword with no practical implication", "Only government officials' convenience should be considered in design, never citizens'"], answer: 1, exp: "Technology deployed without genuine attention to citizen usability and needs risks failing to deliver its intended real-world benefit." },
  { id: "smc-31", topic: "eg-smart", q: "A key reason integrated smart-city data platforms must include strong data governance/privacy safeguards is:", options: ["Data governance and privacy have no relevance to smart-city data platforms", "Aggregating large amounts of citizen and city data creates significant privacy and security risk if not properly protected, especially given data spanning multiple sensitive domains (location, utilities, safety)", "All smart-city data should always be made completely public with no restriction", "Privacy concerns only apply to private companies, never government-run smart city systems"], answer: 1, exp: "The very aggregation and integration that makes smart-city platforms powerful also concentrates data in ways that require careful privacy/security governance." },
  { id: "smc-32", topic: "eg-smart", q: "Which best illustrates the connection between 'e-Utilities' and broader smart city sustainability goals?", options: ["e-Utilities have no connection to sustainability whatsoever", "Digital utility monitoring (e.g., real-time water/energy usage data) can help both providers and citizens identify and reduce waste, supporting more sustainable resource use", "e-Utilities always increase resource waste with no possible efficiency benefit", "Sustainability is a concept unrelated to any smart-city utility initiative"], answer: 1, exp: "Better visibility into resource usage, enabled by digital utility monitoring, directly supports more sustainable, efficient resource consumption." },
  { id: "smc-33", topic: "eg-smart", q: "A rural community gaining access to broadband connectivity and a basic set of digital government services would be an example of progress toward:", options: ["A purely urban-only concept with no rural application", "The Smart Village concept — extending connectivity, digital services, and their associated benefits to rural areas", "A concept entirely unrelated to Digital India's goals", "A regression away from digital development"], answer: 1, exp: "This directly reflects the core aim of extending smart, connected service delivery beyond urban centers into rural communities." },
  { id: "smc-34", topic: "eg-smart", q: "Which best explains why 'e-Employment' platforms often include skill-matching or recommendation features, not just job listings?", options: ["Skill matching serves no practical purpose and should be removed", "Helping job seekers identify roles genuinely suited to their skills/interests (and identify skill gaps to address) improves the effectiveness of the platform beyond simply listing available jobs", "Job seekers should be shown completely random job listings with no relevance to their actual skills", "Skill-matching features are only useful for employers, never for job seekers themselves"], answer: 1, exp: "Effective matching (not just raw listings) meaningfully improves outcomes for both job seekers and employers using the platform." },
  { id: "smc-35", topic: "eg-smart", q: "Which of these best represents a realistic public safety application within a smart city?", options: ["A system with no connection to emergency response of any kind", "An integrated emergency response system that uses real-time location/sensor data to help dispatch the nearest available emergency services faster", "A system designed specifically to slow down emergency response times", "A public safety system that operates with absolutely no data or technology involved"], answer: 1, exp: "Faster, more coordinated emergency response through real-time data integration is a common and valuable smart-city public safety application." },
  { id: "smc-36", topic: "eg-smart", q: "Which best describes a key trade-off smart city planners must navigate regarding surveillance-capable technology (e.g., city-wide cameras) deployed for public safety?", options: ["There is no trade-off whatsoever; more surveillance is always purely beneficial with no downside", "Balancing genuine public safety benefits against citizens' privacy rights and the risk of surveillance overreach or misuse", "Public safety technology should never be considered at all, under any circumstances", "Privacy concerns should always be completely dismissed in favor of unlimited surveillance"], answer: 1, exp: "This is a genuine, actively debated policy trade-off in smart-city public safety technology deployment worldwide." },
  { id: "smc-37", topic: "eg-smart", q: "Which best illustrates how 'e-Empowerment' and 'e-Employment' initiatives can work together to benefit a citizen?", options: ["The two initiatives are entirely unrelated with no possible interaction", "A citizen uses a digital literacy program (e-Empowerment) to gain basic digital skills, then uses those skills to access an online job portal and apply for positions (e-Employment)", "e-Empowerment initiatives actively prevent citizens from ever accessing e-Employment platforms", "Only one of the two initiatives can exist at any given time in a given region"], answer: 1, exp: "This illustrates a natural, complementary relationship — empowerment (skills/access) enabling more effective use of employment-focused digital services." },
  { id: "smc-38", topic: "eg-smart", q: "A key long-term goal of smart village and smart city initiatives, taken together, is often described as:", options: ["Deepening the gap between urban and rural quality of life and access to services", "Reducing the urban-rural divide in access to services, connectivity, and opportunity by extending appropriate technology-driven improvements to both contexts", "Ensuring that only urban areas ever receive any technology investment", "Eliminating all distinctions between rural and urban areas by making them physically identical"], answer: 1, exp: "A frequently stated goal is narrowing (not widening) the gap in services/opportunity between urban and rural areas through appropriately tailored digital initiatives." },
  { id: "smc-39", topic: "eg-smart", q: "Which of these best illustrates a smart city initiative directly supporting the 'digital empowerment' pillar of Digital India?", options: ["A city government deliberately restricting citizen internet access", "A city-run digital literacy program teaching residents how to access online government services and use basic digital tools", "A city initiative with no citizen skill-building or education component whatsoever", "An initiative available only to city government employees"], answer: 1, exp: "Direct digital literacy/skill-building for residents is a concrete way smart city programs can support the broader digital empowerment goal." },
  { id: "smc-40", topic: "eg-smart", q: "A well-designed smart city waste-management system using IoT sensors on bins primarily helps by:", options: ["Ensuring waste is never collected under any circumstances", "Enabling more efficient collection routing based on actual fill levels, reducing unnecessary trips and improving overall service efficiency", "Having no measurable impact on collection efficiency", "Only functioning in a single, specific city with no broader applicability"], answer: 1, exp: "Sensor-driven, data-informed routing is a widely cited practical smart-city efficiency improvement for municipal waste management." },
  { id: "smc-41", topic: "eg-smart", q: "Which best explains why 'services on demand' initiatives often need to integrate multiple backend government systems (not just present a single frontend)?", options: ["A single, isolated frontend with no backend integration is always sufficient for meaningful services on demand", "Genuinely convenient, real-time service delivery often requires the frontend to pull data from and coordinate with multiple existing (often previously siloed) backend systems across departments", "Backend integration has no bearing on whether a service can genuinely be delivered 'on demand'", "Government systems should never be integrated with each other under any circumstances"], answer: 1, exp: "A polished citizen-facing interface alone is insufficient if the underlying backend systems and data aren't actually integrated to support real-time, accurate service delivery." },
  { id: "smc-42", topic: "eg-smart", q: "Which of these would be an appropriate metric for evaluating the success of an e-Employment digital platform?", options: ["The number of decorative banner advertisements displayed on the platform", "The rate at which registered job seekers are successfully matched with and placed into suitable employment or relevant training", "A metric entirely unrelated to employment outcomes", "Only the platform's visual color scheme, with no attention to actual outcomes"], answer: 1, exp: "Meaningful evaluation should focus on actual employment/training outcomes achieved, not superficial platform characteristics." },
  { id: "smc-43", topic: "eg-smart", q: "Which best describes the role of public-private partnerships in many real-world smart city implementations?", options: ["Smart cities are always built entirely and exclusively by government with zero private-sector involvement", "Many smart city initiatives involve collaboration between government and private technology/infrastructure providers, combining public objectives with private-sector technical expertise and investment", "Private companies are legally prohibited from any involvement in smart city projects", "Public-private partnerships have no relevance to smart city development"], answer: 1, exp: "Public-private partnership models are commonly used in smart city development, blending public policy goals with private technical/financial capability." },
  { id: "smc-44", topic: "eg-smart", q: "Which best explains why connectivity/infrastructure gaps in rural areas are a particular concern for smart village initiatives?", options: ["Connectivity gaps have no bearing on whether digital services can actually reach and benefit rural residents", "Without reliable connectivity infrastructure, digital services and platforms simply cannot reach the rural residents they are intended to benefit, regardless of how well-designed those services otherwise are", "Rural areas never have any connectivity gaps to begin with", "Smart village initiatives should be designed with zero regard for actual infrastructure availability"], answer: 1, exp: "Connectivity is the foundational prerequisite — no digital service design, however good, can overcome a fundamental lack of underlying infrastructure access." },
  { id: "smc-45", topic: "eg-smart", q: "Which of these best illustrates a genuinely 'citizen-centric' approach to designing a services-on-demand portal?", options: ["Organizing the portal by internal government department names/codes that citizens are unlikely to know", "Organizing the portal around common citizen needs/life events (e.g., 'apply for a passport', 'register a new business') with plain-language navigation", "Requiring citizens to already be IT experts to successfully navigate the portal", "Providing no search or navigation aids of any kind"], answer: 1, exp: "Designing around real citizen needs and using accessible, plain language is a hallmark of genuinely citizen-centric digital service design." },
  { id: "smc-46", topic: "eg-smart", q: "Which best describes why smart city/village programs are frequently evaluated not just on technology deployed, but on actual outcomes achieved?", options: ["Outcomes are irrelevant; only the amount of technology deployed matters", "Deploying technology alone does not guarantee real benefit — genuine success requires measurable improvement in the services, efficiency, or quality of life the technology was intended to improve", "Technology deployment and actual outcomes are always identical with no possible gap between them", "Outcome evaluation is a concept that applies only to unrelated, non-technology programs"], answer: 1, exp: "This distinction (installed technology vs. actual measurable benefit) is central to responsibly evaluating smart-city/village program effectiveness." },
  { id: "smc-47", topic: "eg-smart", q: "Which best illustrates the connection between smart city infrastructure and broader Digital India goals like 'universal digital infrastructure as a core utility'?", options: ["Smart city infrastructure investments have no relationship to the broader Digital India program", "Smart city connectivity and digital infrastructure investments directly contribute to the broader Digital India goal of making reliable digital access as fundamental and universally available as traditional utilities", "Digital India's infrastructure goals apply only to villages, never to cities", "Smart cities are explicitly excluded from the Digital India program"], answer: 1, exp: "Smart city digital infrastructure build-out is a concrete, city-level contribution toward the broader national goal of universal digital infrastructure access." },
  { id: "smc-48", topic: "eg-smart", q: "Which of these best represents a balanced approach to deploying smart-city public safety surveillance technology?", options: ["Deploying unlimited surveillance with zero policy, oversight, or privacy safeguards", "Deploying surveillance-capable technology alongside clear policies on data use/retention, oversight mechanisms, and privacy safeguards proportionate to the stated public safety objective", "Refusing to ever deploy any public-safety-related technology under any circumstances", "Allowing any private company unrestricted access to all surveillance data with no government oversight"], answer: 1, exp: "A balanced approach pairs beneficial technology deployment with appropriate governance, oversight, and privacy safeguards, rather than either extreme (unlimited surveillance or none at all)." },
  { id: "smc-49", topic: "eg-smart", q: "Which best summarizes why 'e-Utilities', 'services on demand', 'e-Empowerment', and 'e-Employment' are often discussed together as related Digital India themes?", options: ["They are entirely unrelated, coincidentally grouped concepts with no common thread", "They collectively represent different facets of using digital technology to directly improve citizens' access to essential services, information, opportunities, and economic participation", "Only one of these four themes has any real-world relevance; the others are purely theoretical", "These themes apply only to government employees, never to ordinary citizens"], answer: 1, exp: "Together, these themes span utilities, general service delivery, empowerment/access, and economic opportunity — complementary dimensions of citizen-focused digital transformation." },
  { id: "smc-50", topic: "eg-smart", q: "Overall, smart village, smart city, e-Utilities, services-on-demand, e-Empowerment, and e-Employment initiatives together illustrate:", options: ["A purely theoretical set of concepts with no practical, real-world implementation", "A practical, multi-faceted approach to using digital technology and data to improve citizens' access to services, information, infrastructure, and economic opportunity, across both urban and rural contexts", "An approach that deliberately excludes rural populations from any benefit", "A single, narrow initiative with no broader connection to citizen welfare"], answer: 1, exp: "These initiatives collectively represent the practical, citizen-facing application of digital transformation principles across diverse urban and rural contexts." },

  // ================= Digital Services, Payments & Ethics (50) =================
  { id: "dsv-01", topic: "eg-services", q: "e-Learning is best described as:", options: ["Only in-person, classroom-based education with no digital component", "Education and training delivered through digital/electronic means, such as online courses, video lectures, and digital learning platforms", "A term unrelated to education entirely", "Only applicable to primary school students, never adults"], answer: 1, exp: "e-Learning broadly covers digitally delivered education, from formal degree programs to informal skill-building courses." },
  { id: "dsv-02", topic: "eg-services", q: "SWAYAM, an Indian government e-learning initiative, primarily provides:", options: ["A physical library with no online component", "Free, online courses (MOOCs) covering school and higher-education subjects, aiming to bridge the digital divide in access to quality education", "A private, paid-only subscription service with no free access", "A platform exclusively for corporate employee training"], answer: 1, exp: "SWAYAM is a flagship Indian government MOOC platform aimed at making quality education broadly and freely accessible." },
  { id: "dsv-03", topic: "eg-services", q: "A key benefit of e-Learning platforms, compared to purely traditional classroom-only education, is:", options: ["They are always strictly worse in every respect with no benefit whatsoever", "They can improve access to education for learners who face geographic, scheduling, or resource constraints, offering flexibility in when/where learning happens", "e-Learning eliminates the need for any learning content or instruction", "They are relevant only to learners who already have advanced degrees"], answer: 1, exp: "Flexibility and broadened access are widely cited advantages of e-Learning, particularly valuable for learners facing traditional barriers to education." },
  { id: "dsv-04", topic: "eg-services", q: "UPI (Unified Payments Interface) is best described as:", options: ["A physical payment card issued by a single bank", "A real-time payment system enabling instant bank-to-bank money transfers via a mobile application, using a simplified interface (e.g., a virtual payment address)", "A cryptocurrency unrelated to traditional banking", "A service available only for business-to-business transactions, never individuals"], answer: 1, exp: "UPI has become one of India's most widely used digital payment mechanisms, enabling instant, simple bank-to-bank transfers." },
  { id: "dsv-05", topic: "eg-services", q: "IMPS (Immediate Payment Service) is:", options: ["A service that only processes payments once per week", "An electronic funds transfer system in India enabling instant interbank money transfer, available 24/7 including holidays", "A physical cash-only payment method", "A service exclusively for international remittances"], answer: 1, exp: "IMPS predates UPI and provides real-time interbank transfers, available around the clock." },
  { id: "dsv-06", topic: "eg-services", q: "NEFT (National Electronic Funds Transfer) and RTGS (Real Time Gross Settlement), as digital payment modes in India, differ primarily in that:", options: ["They are identical with no meaningful distinction", "RTGS is designed for high-value transactions with real-time, individual settlement, while NEFT processes transactions in batches and is typically used for a broader range of transaction values", "NEFT is only used for international transactions, never domestic", "RTGS cannot be used for any bank transfer purpose"], answer: 1, exp: "RTGS settles transactions individually and in real time, generally for larger-value transfers, while NEFT settles in periodic batches." },
  { id: "dsv-07", topic: "eg-services", q: "Mobile wallets, as a mode of digital payment, work by:", options: ["Requiring a physical wallet at every transaction with no digital component", "Allowing users to store money digitally (often pre-loaded) on a mobile app, which can then be used to make payments to merchants or transfer funds", "Only being usable for cryptocurrency transactions", "Functioning identically to a physical bank branch with no app involved"], answer: 1, exp: "Mobile wallets provide a digital store of value that can be used for convenient, app-based payments." },
  { id: "dsv-08", topic: "eg-services", q: "USSD-based mobile banking (like India's *99# service) is particularly valuable because it:", options: ["Requires an expensive smartphone and high-speed internet connection", "Enables basic banking/payment transactions from any mobile phone (including basic feature phones) without requiring internet access, using simple USSD codes", "Only works on the very newest smartphone models", "Cannot be used to check account balances or make transfers"], answer: 1, exp: "USSD-based services extend digital payment access to users without smartphones or reliable internet connectivity, supporting financial inclusion." },
  { id: "dsv-09", topic: "eg-services", q: "The Aadhaar Enabled Payment System (AePS) allows a user to:", options: ["Make payments only using a physical debit card with no biometric involvement", "Perform basic banking transactions (like cash withdrawal or balance inquiry) using their Aadhaar number and biometric authentication (like a fingerprint), without needing a card or PIN", "Only be used by users who have a smartphone with internet access", "Access banking services only in major metropolitan cities"], answer: 1, exp: "AePS uses Aadhaar-linked biometric authentication to enable banking access, particularly valuable for extending services to underserved populations." },
  { id: "dsv-10", topic: "eg-services", q: "BHIM (Bharat Interface for Money) is:", options: ["A physical payment terminal used only by large retailers", "A mobile application developed to facilitate UPI-based digital payments for Indian users, promoting simple, direct bank-to-bank transactions", "A cryptocurrency exchange platform", "A service available only for cross-border remittances"], answer: 1, exp: "BHIM was developed as a UPI-based app to promote widespread adoption of simple, direct digital payments in India." },
  { id: "dsv-11", topic: "eg-services", q: "A digital library, such as India's National Digital Library (NDLI), primarily provides:", options: ["Only physical books with no digital access", "A centralized, searchable, online repository of digital educational content (books, articles, videos, etc.) accessible to users remotely", "A service exclusively for university faculty, with no student access", "Only content in a single specific language with no multilingual support"], answer: 1, exp: "Digital libraries aggregate and provide remote, searchable access to educational content, expanding access beyond physical library locations." },
  { id: "dsv-12", topic: "eg-services", q: "A key benefit of digital libraries over traditional physical-only libraries is:", options: ["They are always strictly worse, offering no advantage whatsoever", "They can be accessed remotely, at any time, by many users simultaneously, without the geographic and physical-copy limitations of a traditional library", "Digital libraries eliminate the need for any actual educational content", "They are relevant only to citizens living in a single specific city"], answer: 1, exp: "Remote, simultaneous, anytime access is a defining advantage of digital libraries over physical-only collections." },
  { id: "dsv-13", topic: "eg-services", q: "'Public safety' applications in the context of digital government services include:", options: ["Only entertainment-related mobile apps with no safety relevance", "Digital emergency-response coordination, disaster management/alert systems, and public-safety-focused citizen reporting tools", "Systems designed specifically to prevent any emergency response from occurring", "A concept with no relevance to e-governance"], answer: 1, exp: "Digital tools supporting faster, better-coordinated emergency response and disaster management are a key public-safety application area of e-governance." },
  { id: "dsv-14", topic: "eg-services", q: "Ethics in e-governance and digital service delivery particularly concerns:", options: ["Purely technical performance metrics with no consideration of fairness or impact", "Ensuring digital initiatives are designed and operated with fairness, transparency, privacy protection, and consideration for their broader social impact", "A concept entirely irrelevant to government technology deployment", "Only applies to private companies, never to government agencies"], answer: 1, exp: "Ethical considerations — fairness, transparency, privacy — are essential to responsible design and operation of digital government services." },
  { id: "dsv-15", topic: "eg-services", q: "Social responsibility, as it relates to digital government initiatives, involves:", options: ["Ignoring the needs of digitally underserved or marginalized populations entirely", "Considering the broader societal impact of digital initiatives, including ensuring inclusive access and avoiding exacerbating existing inequalities (like the digital divide)", "A concept with no practical bearing on how digital services should be designed", "Only benefiting citizens who are already digitally advantaged"], answer: 1, exp: "Socially responsible digital governance actively considers and works to mitigate the risk of leaving underserved populations further behind." },
  { id: "dsv-16", topic: "eg-services", q: "A key ethical consideration when a government deploys large-scale digital payment or identity systems (like Aadhaar-linked services) is:", options: ["No ethical consideration is needed since the system is government-run", "Ensuring appropriate privacy protections, data security, and safeguards against exclusion or misuse, given the sensitivity and scale of the data/services involved", "Making all citizen data completely public with no restriction", "Ethics only applies to purely voluntary, opt-in systems, never widely adopted infrastructure"], answer: 1, exp: "The scale and sensitivity of national digital identity/payment infrastructure make careful privacy, security, and inclusion safeguards especially important." },
  { id: "dsv-17", topic: "eg-services", q: "Digital payment modes' contribution to financial inclusion is best illustrated by:", options: ["Digital payments being accessible only to those who already have full access to traditional banking services", "Services like AePS and USSD-based banking extending basic financial transaction capability to populations who may lack smartphones, reliable internet, or nearby bank branches", "Digital payments having no relationship to financial inclusion whatsoever", "Digital payment modes deliberately excluding rural or underserved populations"], answer: 1, exp: "By reducing dependence on smartphones, internet, and physical bank branches, these specific digital payment modes directly support broader financial inclusion." },
  { id: "dsv-18", topic: "eg-services", q: "Which best explains why offering multiple different digital payment modes (UPI, IMPS, NEFT/RTGS, USSD, AePS, wallets) matters for a diverse population like India's?", options: ["Offering multiple modes serves no practical purpose and should be avoided in favor of a single option", "Different modes suit different user contexts (smartphone vs. basic phone, transaction size, urgency, banking access), so a range of options helps ensure broader accessibility across a diverse population", "All citizens have identical needs, so only one single payment mode is ever necessary", "Multiple payment modes exist purely by historical accident with no practical rationale"], answer: 1, exp: "A diverse population with varying device access, connectivity, and banking relationships benefits from having multiple appropriately-suited digital payment options." },
  { id: "dsv-19", topic: "eg-services", q: "A key public safety and ethics consideration for e-governance systems that collect large amounts of citizen data is:", options: ["Data collection should always be maximized with no limit and no accompanying safeguards", "Balancing the genuine public benefit of data-driven services against citizens' right to privacy, requiring appropriate data minimization, security, and governance safeguards", "Ethics and privacy have no relevance once data has already been collected", "Public safety concerns automatically override any need for privacy protection in every case"], answer: 1, exp: "This benefit-versus-privacy balance is a recurring, genuine ethical tension in designing data-driven public services." },
  { id: "dsv-20", topic: "eg-services", q: "Which best describes the ethical/social responsibility rationale for governments actively promoting digital literacy alongside deploying new digital services?", options: ["Digital literacy has no bearing on whether new digital services actually benefit citizens", "Simply deploying digital services without also building citizens' capacity to use them risks the benefits being captured mainly by those already digitally literate, deepening rather than reducing inequality", "Governments have no responsibility whatsoever for citizens' digital literacy", "Digital literacy promotion is purely a private-sector responsibility with no government role"], answer: 1, exp: "Pairing service deployment with literacy-building is an ethical/social-responsibility response to the risk that new digital services could otherwise disproportionately benefit the already digitally advantaged." },
  { id: "dsv-21", topic: "eg-services", q: "e-Learning platforms supporting features like recorded lectures, quizzes, and discussion forums primarily aim to:", options: ["Make learning as inconvenient and inflexible as possible", "Replicate and, in some ways, enhance aspects of the traditional learning experience (content delivery, assessment, peer interaction) in a flexible, remote-accessible digital format", "Eliminate any form of assessment or interaction entirely", "Only be usable by learners physically present in a single classroom"], answer: 1, exp: "These features aim to provide a comprehensive learning experience — not just passive content delivery — in a flexible digital format." },
  { id: "dsv-22", topic: "eg-services", q: "Which of these best represents the practical difference between NEFT and RTGS in terms of typical use case?", options: ["Both are used interchangeably for identical purposes with no practical difference", "RTGS is typically preferred for large, time-sensitive transactions needing real-time individual settlement, while NEFT is commonly used for a broader range of transaction sizes processed in batches", "NEFT is only used for international wire transfers", "RTGS cannot be used by individual citizens, only by government agencies"], answer: 1, exp: "RTGS's real-time, individual settlement suits urgent, high-value transfers; NEFT's batch processing suits a broader range of everyday transaction needs." },
  { id: "dsv-23", topic: "eg-services", q: "A digital library's cataloging and search functionality primarily helps users by:", options: ["Making it deliberately difficult to find any specific resource", "Enabling users to efficiently search, browse, and discover relevant educational resources across a potentially very large digital collection", "Providing no way to actually locate any specific content", "Only functioning for a single, narrow subject area with no broader coverage"], answer: 1, exp: "Effective search/cataloging is essential for making a large digital collection actually usable and discoverable, not just technically accessible." },
  { id: "dsv-24", topic: "eg-services", q: "Which best explains the ethical importance of transparency in how government digital services collect and use citizen data?", options: ["Transparency has no bearing on citizen trust or the ethical operation of digital services", "Transparency about data collection/use practices helps citizens understand and consent to how their data is handled, supporting accountability and trust in government digital services", "Governments should always keep all data practices completely secret from citizens", "Transparency is only relevant to private companies, never to government agencies"], answer: 1, exp: "Transparency is a foundational ethical principle supporting informed citizen trust and meaningful accountability in digital governance." },
  { id: "dsv-25", topic: "eg-services", q: "A key social responsibility consideration when rolling out mandatory digital-only government services (e.g., eliminating a previous paper-based alternative entirely) is:", options: ["No consideration is needed since digital services are always strictly better for every citizen", "Ensuring citizens who lack digital access or literacy are not entirely excluded from essential services, potentially requiring transitional support or alternative access channels", "Digital-only mandates should always be implemented immediately with zero transition period or support", "Only citizens who already have smartphones deserve consideration"], answer: 1, exp: "Responsible digital transformation considers and mitigates the exclusion risk for citizens who cannot yet fully access digital-only channels." },
  { id: "dsv-26", topic: "eg-services", q: "Which best illustrates the connection between public safety digital systems and citizen trust?", options: ["Public safety technology has no bearing on citizen trust in government", "Well-designed, appropriately governed public safety technology (with clear policies on data use and oversight) can build citizen trust and cooperation, while poorly governed or opaque systems can undermine it", "Citizen trust is entirely unrelated to how public safety technology is implemented or governed", "Public safety systems should always operate with zero transparency to maximize their effectiveness"], answer: 1, exp: "How public safety technology is governed and communicated directly shapes whether it builds or erodes citizen trust." },
  { id: "dsv-27", topic: "eg-services", q: "Which of these would be an example of a digital payment mode particularly well suited to a user with only a basic feature phone and no internet access?", options: ["A smartphone-only mobile wallet app requiring an internet connection", "USSD-based mobile banking (e.g., *99#), which works over a basic phone's standard cellular signal without requiring internet data", "An online-only web-based payment portal accessible only via a computer browser", "A payment mode that requires a smartphone with a specific operating system"], answer: 1, exp: "USSD-based services are specifically designed to work on any mobile phone using basic cellular signaling, without requiring internet connectivity." },
  { id: "dsv-28", topic: "eg-services", q: "A key reason digital libraries often need to address copyright and licensing considerations is:", options: ["Copyright and licensing have no relevance to digital content distribution", "Making educational content freely and widely available digitally must still respect the legal rights of content creators/publishers, requiring appropriate licensing agreements or use of open-access/public-domain material", "Digital libraries should ignore all copyright law entirely", "Only physical books are ever subject to copyright, never digital content"], answer: 1, exp: "Digital libraries must navigate the legal realities of copyright while still working to maximize legitimate access to educational content." },
  { id: "dsv-29", topic: "eg-services", q: "Which best describes why 'various modes of digital payments' (rather than a single mandated mode) is a more socially responsible approach for a country like India?", options: ["A single mandated payment mode would serve every citizen equally well with no downside", "Different citizens have different levels of access to smartphones, internet, and banking relationships, so offering multiple appropriately-suited modes better serves the full diversity of the population, supporting broader financial inclusion", "Offering multiple payment modes is purely a matter of technical convenience with no social responsibility dimension", "Social responsibility has no relevance to payment infrastructure design"], answer: 1, exp: "This diversity of payment modes reflects a deliberate design choice to accommodate India's highly varied population in terms of device access and banking relationships." },
  { id: "dsv-30", topic: "eg-services", q: "Which best illustrates an ethical concern specific to using biometric authentication (like fingerprints, as in AePS) for accessing essential government/financial services?", options: ["Biometric authentication raises no ethical concerns whatsoever", "Concerns can include the security/privacy of stored biometric data, potential authentication failures (e.g., due to worn fingerprints among manual laborers) that could exclude legitimate users, and the consequences of biometric data misuse or breach", "Biometric authentication is always perfectly reliable for every single user with zero failure risk", "There is no meaningful difference in ethical stakes between biometric and password-based authentication"], answer: 1, exp: "Biometric authentication for essential services raises real, actively debated ethical/practical concerns around data security, inclusion, and failure modes, which responsible system design must address." },
  { id: "dsv-31", topic: "eg-services", q: "Which best describes the relationship between digital libraries and e-Learning platforms?", options: ["They are entirely unrelated with no practical overlap or complementarity", "Digital libraries often provide reference/supplementary content that complements structured e-Learning courses, together supporting a broader digital education ecosystem", "Digital libraries and e-Learning platforms can never be used by the same person for related purposes", "Only one of the two can exist at any given time in a given education system"], answer: 1, exp: "These two categories of digital educational resources naturally complement each other within a broader digital education ecosystem." },
  { id: "dsv-32", topic: "eg-services", q: "Which of these best represents responsible design of a digital emergency-alert/disaster-management system?", options: ["A system that sends alerts only through a single channel (e.g., a smartphone app) with no alternative for those without smartphones", "A system that uses multiple redundant channels (SMS, app notifications, sirens, radio/TV) to maximize the chance that all citizens, regardless of device access, receive critical safety information", "A system deliberately designed to reach the fewest possible number of citizens", "A system that only operates during business hours, never during actual emergencies"], answer: 1, exp: "Redundant, multi-channel alerting is a responsible design practice ensuring critical public safety information reaches the broadest possible population, including those without smartphones." },
  { id: "dsv-33", topic: "eg-services", q: "Which best describes the ethical principle of 'accountability' as applied to government digital services?", options: ["Accountability means no one is ever responsible for how a digital service performs or is used", "Accountability means clear ownership and responsibility for a digital service's performance, data handling, and outcomes, including mechanisms for citizens to raise concerns or seek redress", "Accountability only applies to private-sector digital services, never to government", "Accountability is a purely abstract concept with no practical implementation"], answer: 1, exp: "Clear accountability — including citizen redress mechanisms — is a core ethical requirement for responsibly operated government digital services." },
  { id: "dsv-34", topic: "eg-services", q: "Which best explains why 'ethics' and 'social responsibility' are commonly grouped together with technical topics like digital payments and public safety in an e-Governance curriculum?", options: ["Ethics and social responsibility have no practical connection to how digital payment or public safety systems should actually be designed and operated", "Effectively deploying digital payment, safety, and other citizen-facing systems requires considering not just technical functionality but also fairness, privacy, accessibility, and broader societal impact — making ethics and social responsibility integral, not separate, considerations", "These topics are grouped together purely by coincidence with no substantive relationship", "Ethics only matters after a digital system has already failed, never during its design"], answer: 1, exp: "Responsible e-governance design integrates ethical and social-responsibility considerations directly into technical system design, rather than treating them as an afterthought." },
  { id: "dsv-35", topic: "eg-services", q: "Which of these best illustrates 'public safety, ethics and social responsibility' considerations working together in a single real-world e-governance example?", options: ["A disaster-alert system that reaches only smartphone users in the capital city, ignores rural areas, and shares alert-recipient location data publicly with no privacy protection", "A disaster-alert system designed to reach citizens through multiple accessible channels, with data handled under clear privacy safeguards, and with mechanisms to evaluate whether it's reaching underserved populations effectively", "A system with no consideration of who it does or doesn't reach", "A system that intentionally excludes rural or low-income populations from receiving any safety information"], answer: 1, exp: "This example integrates broad accessibility (social responsibility), privacy safeguards (ethics), and effective emergency communication (public safety) together, exactly as responsible e-governance design should." },
  { id: "dsv-36", topic: "eg-services", q: "Which best explains why digital payment systems in India commonly implement transaction limits and fraud-monitoring safeguards?", options: ["Such safeguards serve no practical purpose and should be removed", "To help protect users from unauthorized transactions, fraud, and financial loss, balancing the convenience of instant digital payments against legitimate security risk", "Transaction limits exist purely to make digital payments as inconvenient as possible with no security rationale", "Fraud is not a real concern for digital payment systems"], answer: 1, exp: "These safeguards reflect a deliberate, ethically motivated balance between payment convenience and protecting users from financial harm." },
  { id: "dsv-37", topic: "eg-services", q: "Which best describes why digital library initiatives like NDLI often specifically emphasize multilingual content?", options: ["Multilingual support has no bearing on the accessibility or inclusiveness of a digital library", "Given India's linguistic diversity, providing content in multiple languages significantly broadens genuine access and usability of digital library resources across the population, rather than serving only speakers of a single language", "Digital libraries should only ever be available in a single language, with no exception", "Multilingual support is purely a cosmetic feature with no real accessibility benefit"], answer: 1, exp: "Multilingual content directly supports the social-responsibility goal of making digital resources genuinely accessible across a linguistically diverse population." },
  { id: "dsv-38", topic: "eg-services", q: "Which of these best represents a socially responsible approach to designing an e-Learning platform intended to reach economically disadvantaged learners?", options: ["Requiring expensive premium hardware and a paid subscription with no free-access option", "Offering free or low-cost access, optimizing content for low-bandwidth connections, and providing content in accessible, relevant languages/formats", "Assuming all learners already have unlimited high-speed internet and the latest devices", "Deliberately designing content that is difficult for less digitally experienced learners to use"], answer: 1, exp: "These design choices directly address the practical barriers economically disadvantaged learners are more likely to face, reflecting socially responsible platform design." },
  { id: "dsv-39", topic: "eg-services", q: "Which best explains the ethical significance of ensuring digital payment/service failures (e.g., a failed transaction) are handled fairly and transparently?", options: ["Transaction failures have no ethical significance and can be handled arbitrarily", "Since citizens increasingly depend on digital payment/service systems for essential transactions, fair, transparent handling of failures/disputes (e.g., clear reversal/refund processes) is important for protecting users and maintaining trust in the system", "Failures should always be blamed entirely on the user with no recourse offered", "Transparency about failures should be actively avoided to protect the system's reputation"], answer: 1, exp: "As digital systems become essential infrastructure, fair and transparent failure handling is an ethical obligation, not just a technical nicety." },
  { id: "dsv-40", topic: "eg-services", q: "Which best summarizes the overall relationship between e-Learning, digital payments, digital libraries, public safety, and ethics/social responsibility as a set of related e-Governance topics?", options: ["They are a random, unrelated collection of topics with no unifying theme", "They together represent key digital services delivered to citizens (education, payments, information access, safety), all of which must be designed and governed with careful attention to ethics and social responsibility to genuinely and fairly benefit the population", "Only the technical/functional aspects of these services matter; ethical considerations are irrelevant to all of them", "These topics apply only to a narrow, privileged subset of the population, by design"], answer: 1, exp: "This set of topics spans core citizen-facing digital services, unified by the need for ethically responsible, broadly inclusive design and governance." },
  { id: "dsv-41", topic: "eg-services", q: "Which of these best illustrates the concept of 'digital inclusion' as it relates to digital payment modes?", options: ["Designing a payment system that works only for users with the newest smartphones and unlimited data plans", "Offering a range of payment options (from smartphone apps to USSD-based basic-phone services) so that citizens across different levels of device access and connectivity can all participate in the digital payment ecosystem", "Digital inclusion is an irrelevant concept with no bearing on payment system design", "Ensuring that only urban, already-banked citizens can use digital payment services"], answer: 1, exp: "Digital inclusion is precisely about designing systems so that citizens with varying levels of access and resources can all meaningfully participate." },
  { id: "dsv-42", topic: "eg-services", q: "Which best describes a realistic ethical trade-off a government might face when deciding whether to make a citizen service exclusively digital versus retaining a parallel non-digital option?", options: ["There is no trade-off; exclusively digital services are always strictly better for every citizen with no downside", "Exclusively digital delivery can improve efficiency/cost and speed for most users, but risks excluding citizens who lack digital access or literacy, requiring a careful, often gradual, transition approach", "Non-digital service options should always be maintained forever with no consideration of efficiency", "This trade-off has no real-world relevance to actual government policy decisions"], answer: 1, exp: "This efficiency-versus-inclusion trade-off is a genuine, actively navigated policy consideration in real-world digital government transitions." },
  { id: "dsv-43", topic: "eg-services", q: "Which best explains why 'social responsibility' considerations extend beyond just designing accessible technology, to also include ongoing monitoring/evaluation?", options: ["Monitoring and evaluation serve no useful purpose once a digital service has been launched", "Even a well-designed service can fail to reach or benefit its intended population in practice, so ongoing monitoring/evaluation helps identify and address gaps, ensuring the service continues to deliver on its social responsibility goals over time", "Social responsibility is fully achieved the moment a service is launched, with no need for any follow-up", "Monitoring and evaluation are relevant only to private-sector products, never government digital services"], answer: 1, exp: "Genuine social responsibility requires an ongoing commitment to verifying real-world impact, not just a one-time design intention." },
  { id: "dsv-44", topic: "eg-services", q: "Which of these best represents an ethically sound approach to using citizen data collected through digital public-safety systems for a secondary, unrelated purpose (e.g., marketing)?", options: ["Freely repurposing the data for any secondary use with no restriction or citizen awareness", "Generally avoiding repurposing citizen data collected for one specific purpose (like public safety) for unrelated purposes without appropriate legal basis, transparency, and/or consent", "Actively selling citizen safety data to any interested third party with no oversight", "Data purpose has no ethical relevance once data has already been collected"], answer: 1, exp: "This reflects the broader data-protection principle of purpose limitation, an ethical safeguard against data collected for one purpose being inappropriately repurposed for another." },
  { id: "dsv-45", topic: "eg-services", q: "Which best illustrates why offering e-Learning content in accessible formats (e.g., captions, adjustable playback speed, mobile-friendly design) matters from a social responsibility perspective?", options: ["Accessibility features have no bearing on whether e-Learning content genuinely reaches and benefits a diverse population of learners", "Accessible design broadens genuine usability of e-Learning content for learners with disabilities, varying connectivity, or different device types, supporting more equitable educational access", "Accessibility features should be considered only for a small elite group of users, with no broader relevance", "e-Learning content should be designed exclusively for learners with the most advanced technical setups"], answer: 1, exp: "Thoughtful accessibility design directly supports the broader goal of equitable digital education access across a diverse population of learners." },
  { id: "dsv-46", topic: "eg-services", q: "Which best explains the connection between digital payment adoption and reduced reliance on cash transactions?", options: ["Digital payment adoption has no relationship to cash usage patterns", "Widespread digital payment adoption can reduce reliance on physical cash, offering benefits like improved transaction traceability and convenience, though it also raises considerations around ensuring continued access for those less able to use digital payments", "Digital payments always completely eliminate the use of physical cash with no transition considerations needed", "Cash and digital payments serve identical purposes with no meaningful trade-off between them"], answer: 1, exp: "The shift toward digital payments brings real benefits alongside genuine inclusion considerations for those who may still depend on cash." },
  { id: "dsv-47", topic: "eg-services", q: "Which of these best represents a responsible approach to public safety technology that uses citizen-reported data (e.g., a crime or hazard reporting app)?", options: ["Publishing all reporter identities publicly with no privacy protection whatsoever, discouraging future reporting", "Protecting reporter privacy/anonymity where appropriate, verifying reports responsibly, and ensuring the system doesn't enable misuse (e.g., false or malicious reports causing harm)", "Ignoring every citizen report received through the system with no follow-up whatsoever", "Requiring citizens to pay a fee before their safety report will be reviewed at all"], answer: 1, exp: "Responsible design of citizen-reporting public safety tools must balance genuine utility against privacy protection and safeguards against misuse." },
  { id: "dsv-48", topic: "eg-services", q: "Which best summarizes why understanding 'various modes of digital payments' matters practically for someone working in IT/e-governance?", options: ["Different payment modes have no practical relevance to designing or evaluating citizen-facing financial services", "Understanding the strengths, limitations, and appropriate use cases of different payment modes (UPI, IMPS, NEFT/RTGS, USSD, AePS, wallets) helps in designing inclusive, appropriate financial digital services for a diverse population", "Only one single payment mode is ever relevant in any real-world government or enterprise context", "This knowledge is relevant only to private banks, never to government digital service design"], answer: 1, exp: "Practical familiarity with the digital payment landscape directly informs better design and evaluation of citizen-facing financial digital services." },
  { id: "dsv-49", topic: "eg-services", q: "Which best explains why ethics and social responsibility are considered ongoing, evolving concerns in digital governance, rather than a one-time checklist?", options: ["Once a digital service is initially designed ethically, no further attention to ethics is ever needed again", "As technology, citizen needs, and societal context evolve, new ethical considerations and social responsibility challenges continually emerge, requiring ongoing attention rather than a single fixed assessment", "Ethics and social responsibility considerations never change once established, making ongoing review unnecessary", "Ethical considerations apply only to the initial launch of a digital service, never to its ongoing operation"], answer: 1, exp: "Given how rapidly technology and its societal implications evolve, digital governance ethics requires continuous attention, not a one-time evaluation." },
  { id: "dsv-50", topic: "eg-services", q: "Overall, e-Learning, digital payment modes, digital libraries, public safety applications, and the accompanying ethics/social responsibility considerations together illustrate:", options: ["A disconnected set of unrelated government technology projects with no common purpose", "How digital government services aim to expand citizens' access to education, financial services, information, and safety — while requiring deliberate, ongoing attention to fairness, privacy, accessibility, and broader social impact to genuinely benefit the whole population", "An approach that deliberately prioritizes technology deployment over any consideration of citizen impact or fairness", "A set of topics relevant only to citizens who are already digitally advantaged"], answer: 1, exp: "Together, these topics represent the practical delivery of essential digital citizen services, unified by the need for genuinely responsible, inclusive design and governance." }

);
